\h 2 Chronicles
\toc1 The second Sequential Account
\toc2 2 Chronicles
\mt2 The second
\mt1 Sequential Account
\mt3 commonly called
\mt2 2 Chronicles
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\s1 The requesting of Solomon of wisdom
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Kgs 3:1-15)
\p
\v 1 David’s son Solomon became established over his kingdom, and his god, Yahweh, was with him and made him very powerful.
\p
\v 2 Solomon spoke to all of Israel, to the army commanders of thousands and of hundreds, to the judges, to all of Israel’s leaders, including the clan leaders.
\v 3 So Solomon led the whole assembly to the worship place at Gibeon, because the sacred tent was there that Yahweh’s servant Moses had made in the wilderness.
\v 4 David had brought God’s box uphill from Kiryat-Yearim, \add and\add* he’d erected a tent for it in Jerusalem.
\v 5 The bronze altar made by Betsalel (son of Uri, son of Hur) was there in front of Yahweh’s tent, and Solomon and the assembly requested Yahweh’s guidance there.
\v 6 So Solomon went up to the bronze altar there at the sacred tent, and in front of Yahweh, he sacrificed a thousand burnt offerings on it.
\p
\v 7 That night, God appeared to Solomon and told him, “Ask for what \add you want\add* me to give you.”
\p
\v 8 “You showed outstanding loyal commitment to my father David,” Solomon replied to God, “and you’ve made me king in his place.
\v 9 Now, Yahweh God, may your promise to my father David be confirmed, because you yourself have made me king over a people group as numerous as the dust on the ground.
\v 10 Give me wisdom and knowledge now as I come and go in front of these people, because who could make \add good\add* judgements for this huge people group of yours?”
\p
\v 11 “Because that was in your heart,” God replied to Solomon “and you didn’t ask for wealth, riches, or fame, or the lives of those who hate you, and also you didn’t ask for a long life, but you’ve asked for wisdom and knowledge for yourself to help you lead my people that I’ve made you king over,
\v 12 then I’ll give you \add that\add* wisdom and knowledge, \add along with\add* wealth and riches and fame. You’ll have more than any king that preceded you or any that follows.”
\s1 Solomon’s honour and wealth
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Kgs 10:26-29)
\p
\v 13 Then Solomon left the sacred tent at Gibeon and returned to Jerusalem, where he reigned over Israel.
\v 14 Then Solomon built up his chariots and horsemen, and soon had 1,400 chariots and 12,000 horsemen, which he stationed in the chariot cities and with him in Jerusalem.
\v 15 The king made silver and gold in Jerusalem as \add common\add* as stones, and he made \add imported\add* cedar \add timber\add* as \add common\add* as the \add local\add* sycamore trees that were plentiful in the lowlands.
\v 16 Solomon’s horses were imported from Egypt and from the traders in Kue \add (in modern southern Türkiye/Turkey)\add*.
\v 17 Egyptian chariots cost six hundred silver coins and horses cost one hundred and fifty. (The Hittite and Aramean kings were able to get them for the same price.)
\c 2
\s1 Preparations for temple construction
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Kgs 5:1-18)
\p
\v 1 Then Solomon decided to build a temple to honour Yahweh, as well as a palace for himself,
\v 2 so he conscripted seventy thousand men as porters, eighty thousand as stone-cutters in the hillside quarries, and 3,600 supervisors.
\p
\v 3 Solomon contacted King Huram in Tyre, saying, “In my father David’s time, you sent cedar \add timber\add* to him so he could build himself a palace to live in.
\v 4 Listen, I’m building a temple to honour my god Yahweh, to consecrate to him. There we’ll burn spiced incense for him, plus we’ll have the regular \add bread\add* display, and burnt offerings in the morning and in the evening, on rest days and on the new moons, and at Yahweh our god’s other appointed times. Israel is required to do that forever.
\v 5 We want to build an impressive building, because our god is more powerful than all \add the other\add* gods.
\v 6 But who could stay strong enough to build a house for him, since the sky and the heavens of the heavens can’t contain him? And who am I that I could build a house for him—maybe I’m good enough to burn incense for him?
\v 7 So then, send me a skilled man who can work in gold, and in silver, and in bronze, and in iron, and with purple, red, and blue \add threads\add*, and who knows how to engrave. \add He’ll work\add* with the skilled \add craftsmen\add* who my father David appointed and who are with me \add here\add* in Jerusalem and across Judah.
\v 8 Also, send me cedar, cypress, and algum timber from \add there in\add* Lebanon, because I myself know that your servants know how to cut the trees from Lebanon’s \add forests\add*. And listen, my servants will help your servants
\v 9 so they can supply me with plenty of timber, because the temple I’m building will be large and beautiful.
\v 10 I’ll pay your wood-cutters three thousand tonnes of ground wheat, and the same amount of barley, and four hundred thousand litres of wine, and the same amount of \add olive\add* oil.
\p
\v 11 King Huram of Tyre wrote back to \add King\add* Solomon, “Yahweh has appointed you as king over his people because he loves them.
\v 12 Blessed be Israel’s god Yahweh who made the heavens and the earth, who’s given King David a wise son with insight and understanding, who’ll build a temple for Yahweh and a palace for his kingdom.
\p
\v 13 “I’ll send Huram-Avi to you—he’s a skilled craftsman.
\v 14 His mother’s from the tribe of Dan, and his father’s from Tyre. He knows how to work in gold and silver, in bronze and iron, in stone and in timber, with purple and blue \add threads\add*, with fine \add white\add* linen and with crimson \add thread\add*, and do any kind of engraving. He can follow any pattern that’s given to him by your designers, and the designers of my master, David, your father.
\v 15 So \add then go ahead\add* and send the wheat, barley, \add olive\add* oil, and wine that you promised to send to us,
\v 16 then we’ll cut the trees you need from Lebanon and raft them to Joppa by sea. The responsibility of getting them up the hills to Jerusalem will be yours.
\s1 Temple construction begins
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Kgs 6:1-38)
\p
\v 17 Solomon had all the foreign men living in Israel counted (similar to what his father David had done), and it came to 153,600.
\v 18 He conscripted seventy thousand as porters, eighty thousand as stone-cutters, and 3,600 as supervisors to keep the others working.
\c 3
\p
\v 1 Then Solomon began to build Yahweh’s temple on Mt. Moriyah in Jerusalem, where Yahweh had appeared to his father David. It was at Ornan the Jebusite’s threshing floor where David had planned for it,
\v 2 and they began the construction in early-May of the fourth year of Solomon’s reign.\f + \fr 3:2 \ft Probably in 966 B.C.\f*
\v 3 The temple foundation was twenty-seven metres long and nine metres wide,
\v 4 and the nine metre long front entrance porch was nine metres high.\f + \fr 3:4 \ft Most modern translations assume that the Hebrew ‘fifty-five metres high’ is an error.\f* Solomon overlaid the inside \add walls\add* with pure gold.
\v 5 For the main building, he overlaid it first with cypress planking, then with pure gold, then carved palm trees and chains \add along the walls\add*.
\v 6 The gold \add they used\add* came from Parvayim.\f + \fr 3:6 \ft Probably somewhere south of Israel in the Aravi peninsula, but possibly to the east.\f* The main building was \add also\add* overlaid with valuable gemstones.
\v 7 He overlaid the temple beams, thresholds, walls, and doors with gold, and he engraved winged creatures on the walls.
\p
\v 8 Then he made the most sacred room \add inside\add* the temple. It was nine metres long \add (the full width of the temple)\add* and the same width. He overlaid it with thirty kilograms of pure gold \add (beaten into thin sheets)\add*.
\v 9 Each gold nail weighed half a kilogram, and he also overlaid the upper rooms with gold.
\p
\v 10 He cast \add molten metal\add* into two winged creatures in the most sacred room and overlaid them with gold.
\v 11 Each of the creatures had two wings, each 2.3 metres long. One of the wings reached the temple wall, and the other wing was stretched out to touch the other creature’s wingtip,
\v 12 and the same for its companion,
\v 13 \add so\add* the total span of the two winged creatures was nine metres. They \add were made\add* standing on their feet, and \add placed\add* with their faces towards \add the opening into\add* the main hall.
\v 14 The curtain \add separating the two rooms\add* was made from fine-linen with blue, purple, and red thread, and with winged creatures \add embroidered\add* onto it.
\s1 The two bronze pillars
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Kgs 7:15-22)
\p
\v 15 For the front of the temple he made two sixteen metre tall pillars, which each had a ‘capital’ topping that was another two metres high.
\v 16 \add Ornamental\add* chains were made for the inner sanctuary and to go around the tops of the pillars, and there were one hundred pomegranate \add models\add* hanging on the chains.
\v 17 There was one pillar on each side of the \add east-facing\add* entrance—the lefthand one was named Yakin and the righthand one was named Boaz.
\c 4
\s1 The temple equipment
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Kgs 7:23-51)
\p
\v 1 Then he made a square bronze altar, nine metres on each side and 4.5m high,
\v 2 and he made the large round metal \add water tank\add* (\add called\add* ‘The Sea’), which was 4.5m in diameter and over two metres high.
\v 3 Below the outer rim were two rows of small ornamental bulls spaced 45cm apart, which were part of the same casting.
\v 4 Supporting ‘The Sea’ were twelve large cast bulls—facing outwards, with three facing each of the four compass points.
\v 5 The sides of the tank were eight centimeters thick. The tank’s brim was fashioned like the brim of a cup, resembling a lily blossom. The tank held sixty-six thousand litres of water.
\v 6 Then he made ten washbasins, placing five on each side for the priests to rinse the burnt offerings in. The priests used ‘The Sea’ to wash in.
\p
\v 7 He made the ten gold lampstands as per their design, and placed them in the temple with five on each side.
\v 8 He made ten tables and \add also\add* placed them in the temple with five on each side, plus he made a hundred gold basins.
\p
\v 9 He made the courtyard for the priests, as well as the large courtyard with its doors overlaid with bronze,
\v 10 and he placed ‘The Sea’ in the southeast corner.
\p
\v 11 Meanwhile, Huram made the tubs and shovels \add (for the ashes from the altar)\add* and \add other small\add* bowls.
\p So Huram finished the work for God’s temple that King Solomon had assigned to him:
\v 12 the two pillars, the bowl-shaped capitals on top of each pillar, the two sets of latticework to cover those two capitals’ bowls,
\v 13 and the four hundred pomegranates for the latticework—two rows of pomegranates to cover each of the two bowls of the capitals that decorated the tops of the pillars.
\v 14 He also made the washbasins and their stands,
\v 15 ‘The Sea’ and the twelve bulls that hold it up,
\v 16 and the tubs and shovels, the forks, and other utensils.
\p Those were all items made of polished bronze that Huram-Avi made for King Solomon for Yahweh’s temple.
\p
\v 17 The king had them cast in the clay between Sukot and Tseredah \add down\add* by the Jordan \add river\add*.
\v 18 Solomon had so many items made that they didn’t even try to weigh all the bronze.
\p
\v 19 Solomon had all the items for God’s temple made, including the gold altar and the tables for displaying the sacred bread,
\v 20 the pure gold lampstands and their lamps for the inner sanctuary according to their designs,
\v 21 the pure gold flower \add ornaments\add*, lamps, tongs,
\v 22 the pure gold lamp trimmers, the basins and spoons, and the fire pans. Also the temple entrance, its inner doors into the very sacred room, and the \add outer\add* temple doors were \add all\add* gold.
\c 5
\p
\v 1 After all the work which Solomon did for Yahweh’s temple was completed, Solomon brought in his father David’s sacred things. He put the gold and silver, and all the \add other\add* items, in the temple treasuries.
\s1 Bringing the sacred box into the temple
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Kgs 8:1-9)
\p
\v 2 Then Solomon assembled the Israelite elders, and all the heads of the tribes, the Israelite clan leaders, in Jerusalem, to bring the box containing Yahweh’s agreement up \add the hill\add* from David’s city, that is, Zion.
\v 3 So in October, all those Israelite leaders gathered to be with the king for a feast,
\v 4 and once everyone was there, the Levites lifted up the \add sacred\add* box
\v 5 and carried it up \add towards the temple\add*. The priests (who are also Levites) carried it, along with the \add sacred\add* tent and everything that had been in it.
\v 6 Then King Solomon, and all the assembled Israelites with him, gathered in front of the box, sacrificing sheep and cattle which were too many to count.
\v 7 The priests then took the box containing Yahweh’s agreement in to its place in the house’s inner room (the holiest place)—to under the winged creatures,
\v 8 whose outstretched wings would now be over the \add sacred\add* box and even over the top of its carrying poles.
\v 9 Those long poles stuck out and could be seen from the front of that inner room, but couldn’t be seen from the main room, and they’re still there to this day.
\v 10 There was nothing in the box except the two \add stone\add* tablets that Moses had put in at Horeb, which \add represented the agreement that\add* Yahweh had made with the Israelites after they’d left Egypt.
\s1 Yahweh’s splendour
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 11 Then the priests left the holy place in the temple. All the priests who were there consecrated themselves to do the priestly duties, irrespective of which division they were part of.
\v 12 All the Levites who were singers (Asaf, Heyman, Yedutun, and their sons and their brothers) stood on the east side of the altar. They were dressed in fine linen, and had cymbals, and harps and lyres, along with 120 priests playing trumpets.
\v 13 The trumpeters and singers performed together to praise and thank Yahweh, with the trumpets and cymbals on the highlights of the song to praise Yahweh:
\q1 “Yahweh is good to us,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.”
\p Then Yahweh’s temple filled with a cloud,
\v 14 and the priests weren’t able to stay on their feet and continue ministering, because Yahweh’s brilliance had filled the temple.
\c 6
\s1 Solomon’s blessing
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Kgs 8:12-21)
\p
\v 1 Then Solomon prayed, “Yahweh, you said that you would live in a dark cloud,
\v 2 but I’ve built a beautiful residence for you—a place for you to live in forever.”
\p
\v 3 Then, while all the people stood \add there\add*, Solomon turned toward them and spoke a blessing over them,
\v 4 saying, “Yahweh, Israel’s god, is blessed, who spoke physically to my father David, and then caused it to actually happen, when he said \add back then\add*,
\v 5 ‘When I brought my people out of Egypt, I hadn’t chosen a city from all of Israel’s tribes as a place to build a house so my name could be there. Nor had I chosen a man to be leader over my people, Israel,
\v 6 but \add now\add* I’ve chosen Jerusalem to be my place of residence, and I’ve chosen \add you\add*, David, to be over my people, Israel.’
\p
\v 7 “My father David wanted to build a temple for Israel’s god Yahweh,
\v 8 but Yahweh told him, ‘It’s good that you wanted to build a house to honour me,
\v 9 but you won’t be the one to build it. It’ll be your own biological son who’ll build the temple to honour my reputation.’
\p
\v 10 “Now Yahweh has fulfilled what he said, and I’m here sitting on Israel’s throne instead of my father David, just as Yahweh said, and I’ve had this temple built to honour Israel’s god Yahweh.
\v 11 I’ve placed the \add sacred\add* box in there—the box that contains the agreement Yahweh made with Israel’s descendants.”
\s1 Solomon’s prayer
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Kgs 8:22-53)
\p
\v 12 Then Solomon stood in front of the altar to Yahweh in front of all the assembled Israelites, and spread out his hands,
\v 13 because he’d made a bronze platform and had it placed in the centre of the courtyard. The square platform was just over two metres on each side, and stood just over a metre off the ground. Firstly he stood on it, then he knelt down in front of all the assembled Israelites and spread his hands out to the sky
\v 14 and said, “Yahweh, Israel’s god, there isn’t any god like you in the heavens or on the earth—one who keeps his agreement and \add shows\add* loyal commitment to your servants who are walking with total integrity as you watch them.
\v 15 Everything you verbally told my father David that you’d do, you’ve used your power to make it all happen, including this today.
\v 16 And now, Yahweh, Israel’s god, keep \add another promise you made\add* to your servant David my father, when you told him, ‘There’ll always be one of your descendants sitting on Israel’s throne, as long as they obey my instructions just like you have.’
\v 17 So now, Yahweh, Israel’s god, may what you said to your servant, to David, be confirmed.
\p
\v 18 “But will God really live with humans \add here\add* on the earth? Wow, the skies and the heavens of the heavens can’t contain you—how much less this house that I’ve built.
\v 19 Nevertheless Yahweh my god, listen to your servant’s prayer and request and take notice.
\v 20 Watch over this building where you said you’d be present, and hear my prayers when your servant prays towards this place.
\v 21 Listen to what your servant and your Israelite people are requesting when they pray towards this place. Listen from where you live in the heavens, and hear and forgive.
\p
\v 22 “If someone \add is accused of\add* wronging their neighbour, and is forced to make a sworn statement in front of your altar in this temple,
\v 23 then listen from the heavens and decide who’s telling the truth. Then punish the guilty party as they deserve, and do good to the other side as a reward for their innocence.
\p
\v 24 “And if your Israelite people are defeated by their enemies because they’ve disobeyed you, but they return \add to you\add* and confess, and pray, and in this place they request your favour,
\v 25 then listen to them from the heavens and forgive your Israelite people’s disobedience, and bring them back to this land that you gave to their ancestors and to them.
\p
\v 26 “When the clouds hold back and give no rain because your Israelite people have disobeyed you, but then they pray towards this place and acknowledge you and turn away from their disobedience because you’ve punished them,
\v 27 then listen to them from the heavens and forgive the disobedience of your servants and your Israelite people. Teach them the right way to live their lives, and give rain to your land which you gave your people as a permanent inheritance.
\p
\v 28 “If this land \add suffers\add* from famine, or if there’s blight or mildew, or locusts or grasshoppers, deadly diseases or sickness, or if an enemy \add army\add* surrounds them,
\v 29 then if any person, or all your Israelite people, stretch out their hands towards this temple with their prayers and requests because they each acknowledge their individual pain and suffering,
\v 30 then listen to them from where you live there in the heavens, and forgive them. Reward each person according to their behaviour, because you know people’s motives—only you know what’s in people’s minds.
\v 31 \add Do that\add* so they’ll respect you and live how you want them to, as they live in this country that you gave to our ancestors.
\p
\v 32 “Also, any foreigners who don’t belong to your Israelite people, who come to this land because they’ve heard that you’re very great and powerful, if they turn toward this temple and pray,
\v 33 then listen to them \add as well\add* from where you live there in the heavens. Do whatever that foreigner requests from you, so that all the people groups in the world will learn about your reputation, and honour and obey you like your Israelite people do, and know that this temple that I’ve built is called by your name.
\p
\v 34 “When you send your people off to battle their enemies, and they pray towards this city that you’ve chosen and this temple that I’ve had built \add to honour\add* your name,
\v 35 then listen from the heavens to their prayer and their requests, and uphold their cause.
\p
\v 36 “When they disobey you (and there’s no one who doesn’t), and you’re angry with them and allow an enemy to get the upper hand, and their captors take them to a distant or nearby land as captives,
\v 37 and then in that land where they’re captives, they turn and admit their wickedness and their disobedience, and request your favour,
\v 38 and they turn to you with total sincerity in that other country, and pray towards the land that you gave their ancestors, and the city that you chose, and toward the temple that I’ve built \add to honour\add* your name,
\v 39 then listen from where you live in the heavens to their prayer and their requests, and uphold their cause and forgive your people who’ve disobeyed you.
\p
\v 40 “Now my god, please let your eyes be open and your ears attentive to prayers \add offered towards\add* this place.
\p
\v 41 “So now Yahweh God, along with the \add sacred\add* box representing your strength, come and enter your place for resting. Yahweh God, may your priests be dressed in \add your\add* rescue, and your faithful ones enjoy \add your\add* goodness.
\v 42 Yahweh God, don’t ignore those who you’ve chosen. Remember your servant David’s loyal commitment.”
\c 7
\s1 The temple dedication
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Kgs 8:62-66)
\p
\v 1 When Solomon finished praying, fire came down from the skies and consumed the burnt offering and the sacrifices, and Yahweh’s brilliant light filled the temple.
\v 2 The priests weren’t able to go into Yahweh’s temple because of Yahweh’s brilliant light,
\v 3 and when all the Israelites saw the fire come down and saw the bright light above the temple, they fell \add to their knees with\add* their faces bowed down low to the stone pavement, and worshipped and thanked Yahweh, saying, “He’s certainly good—his loyal commitment \add really\add* will \add continue\add* forever.”
\p
\v 4 Then the king and all the people offered sacrifices to Yahweh—
\v 5 King Solomon sacrificed twenty-two thousand cattle and one hundred and twenty thousand sheep, and \add so\add* the king and all the people dedicated God’s temple.
\v 6 The priests were standing in their positions, as well as the Levites with the instruments King David had made to give thanks to Yahweh. When they assisted David in praising \add Yahweh\add*, \add they would sing\add*, “Yes, his loyal commitment \add continues\add* forever,” and the priests would blow their trumpets in front of them as all the Israelites stood \add there\add*.
\p
\v 7 Then Solomon consecrated the middle of the courtyard in front of Yahweh’s temple building so he could offer burnt offerings and the fat of the peace offerings there, because the bronze altar that he’d made wasn’t able to handle the \add large number of\add* burnt offerings and grain offerings, and the fats.
\p
\v 8 Then Solomon and all of Israel feasted for the \add next\add* seven days—the huge gathering spread from Levo-Hamat \add in the far north\add*, all the way to the Egyptian riverbed \add in the far south\add*.
\v 9 On the eighth day, they had a solemn service, because they’d observed seven days of celebration for the altar with seven days of feasting.
\v 10 Finally, after some three weeks, Solomon sent the people home—they were cheery and encouraged because of the goodness Yahweh had displayed to David and Solomon, and to his Israelite people.
\s1 Yahweh appears to Solomon again
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 11 So Solomon finished Yahweh’s temple and the king’s palace—he’d succeeded in implementing everything he’d envisaged.
\v 12 One night, Yahweh appeared to Solomon in a dream and told him, “I’ve heard your prayer, and I’ve chosen this temple to be the place where my people will offer sacrifices \add to me\add*.
\v 13 If I restrain the skies so there’s no rain, or if I command locusts to devour the land, or if I send disease among my people,
\v 14 then if my people who are known by my name humble themselves and pray, and request my help and turn away from their evil customs, then I myself will hear them from the heavens, and I’ll forgive their sin and heal their land.
\v 15 My eyes will be open and my ears will be attentive to prayers offered towards this place,
\v 16 as I’ve chosen and consecrated this temple to be associated with my name forever—I’ll be permanently present there.
\v 17 Also, if you’ll obey me like your father David did, and do everything that I’ve instructed you, and keep my rules and regulations,
\v 18 then I’ll ensure that your descendants rule your kingdom, just as I promised your father David when I said, ‘You won’t fail to have one of your descendants ruling over Israel.’
\v 19 However, if you \add collectively\add* turn away, and abandon my rules and instructions that I gave you, and decide to serve other gods and worship them,
\v 20 then I’ll remove these people from my land that I gave them. Also this temple that I consecrated for myself, I’ll throw it away where I can’t see it, and I’ll make it an object of scorn and ridicule among all the \add other\add* nations.
\p
\v 21 Then this temple, which everyone admires as they pass by, will be devastated, and people will ask, ‘Why did Yahweh do such terrible things to that temple and to that land?’
\p
\v 22 ‘It happened because they abandoned Yahweh, their ancestors’ god,’ others will reply. ‘He brought them out from \add slavery in\add* Egypt, but then they obtained other gods and worshipped them and served them—that’s why he sent them all this misery.’ ”
\c 8
\s1 King Solomon’s achievements
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Kgs 9:10-28)
\p
\v 1 After the twenty years that it took Solomon to build Yahweh’s temple and his palace,
\v 2 he rebuilt the cities that \add King\add* Huram had given to him, and he got Israelites to live in them.
\p
\v 3 Then Solomon attacked Hamat-Tsovah and captured it.
\v 4 He rebuilt Tadmor in the wilderness, and storage cities in the Hamat \add region\add*.
\v 5 He rebuilt the fortified cities of Upper Beyt-Horon and Lower Beyt-Horon with walls and barred gates,
\v 6 as well as Ba’alat and all the storage cities, and all the cities for Solomon’s chariots and horsemen that he wanted to build in Jerusalem and Lebanon, and throughout his kingdom.
\v 7 All the people who lived in Israel but who were actually remnants of the Hittites, Amorites, Perizim, Hivim, and Jebusites,
\v 8 who were descendants of groups that the Israelites hadn’t completely destroyed, were forced to become Solomon’s labourers, and they still continue in that role.
\v 9 However, Solomon didn’t make the Israelites become labourers, because they became his soldiers, officers, chariot commanders, and horsemen.
\v 10 Some 250 \add Israelites\add* were \add also\add* King Solomon’s chief officials who had authority over the people.
\p
\v 11 Solomon brought \add his wife\add*, Pharaoh’s daughter, up from David’s city to the house he’d built for her, because he said, “My wife mustn’t live in the palace of Israel’s King David, because the places where Yahweh’s box has been are sacred.”
\p
\v 12 Then Solomon sacrificed burnt offerings to Yahweh on the altar that had been built in front of the \add temple’s\add* porch.
\v 13 He followed Moses’ instructions about the daily \add sacrifices\add* for the rest days and the new moons, and for the three appointed occasions each year: the Festival of Flat Bread, the Harvest Festival, and the Celebration Living in Shelters.
\v 14 Then, as his father David had instructed, he assigned the priests to divisions for their various tasks, and the Levites who would praise and minister in front of the priests and help in their daily tasks, and gatekeepers in their divisions at the various gates. \add All\add* that was what David, the man of God, had commanded.
\v 15 The priests and Levites followed the king’s instructions concerning all their duties, as well as \add their supervision of\add* the treasuries.
\p
\v 16 So now, all of Solomon’s work on Yahweh’s temple was completed—from the foundation through to its completion \add and operation\add*.
\p
\v 17 Then Solomon went to Etsyon-Gever and to Eylot on the edge of the ocean, in the Edom region.
\v 18 \add King\add* Huram \add (from Tyre\add* \add )\add* sent ships to him, \add as his\add* servants knew the sea \add well\add*. Then Solomon’s men \add boarded the ships\add* and sailed with them to Ofir, where they obtained fifteen tonnes of gold and brought \add it back\add* to King Solomon.
\c 9
\s1 The queen from Sheba visits
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Kgs 10:1-13)
\p
\v 1 The queen from the Sheba \add region\add* had heard all about Solomon, and she came to Jerusalem with some hard questions. Her party \add included\add* a large number of camels carrying lots of spices and gold and valuable gemstones. She came to Solomon and discussed with him everything that was on her mind.
\v 2 Solomon responded to all her queries, and there wasn’t any subject that he wasn’t able to speak about.
\v 3 When the queen from Sheba saw Solomon’s wisdom, and saw the palace that he’d built
\v 4 and the food at his table, as well as his servants’ stations, his well-dressed ministers standing by, his cup-bearers in their uniforms, and the burnt offerings that he sacrificed at Yahweh’s temple, it took her breath away
\v 5 and she told the king, “Everything I heard in my \add own\add* country about you and your wisdom was true.
\v 6 I didn’t believe what they reported to me until I saw it with my own eyes, and wow, the reports I heard didn’t even cover \em half\em* the extent of your wisdom!
\v 7 Your men are blessed, and \add even\add* your servants are blessed to be able to continually stand in front of you and listen to your wisdom.
\v 8 Your god Yahweh ought to be blessed—the one who delighted in you and placed you on his throne as his king. Your god loved Israel \add and wanted\add* to establish them forever, so he’s placed you over them as king—to give justice and do what’s right.”
\p
\v 9 Then she gave the king four tonnes of gold, a very large quantity of spices, and valuable gemstones. \add Nobody before had ever seen\add* such a large amount of spices as what the queen from Sheba gave to King Solomon.
\p
\v 10 (Also, \add King\add* Huram’s servants \add working together with\add* Solomon’s servants brought gold from Ofir, as well as algum wood and valuable gemstones.
\v 11 The king made the algum wood into staircases for Yahweh’s temple and for the king’s palace, and into lyres and harps for the singers. Nothing like that had been seen before in Judah.)
\p
\v 12 King Solomon gave the queen from Sheba everything she wanted—more than what she’d brought to the king. Then she departed with her servants and went \add back\add* to her country.
\s1 Solomon’s wealth
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Kgs 10:14-25)
\p
\v 13 In one year, Solomon received about twenty tonnes of gold,
\v 14 as well as what the merchants and traders brought in. All the Aravi kings and all the local governors brought gold and silver to Solomon.
\v 15 King Solomon made two hundred large shields of beaten gold—six hundred gold \add coins’ worth\add* of beaten gold went into each shield.
\v 16 Also, three hundred smaller shields—three hundred gold \add coins’ worth\add* of beaten gold went into each shield.
\p
\v 17 Then the king made a large ivory throne and overlaid it with pure gold.
\v 18 There were six steps \add leading up to\add* the throne, and a gold footstool was attached to it. It had an armrest on each side, with a lion \add statue\add* beside each armrest.
\v 19 There were twelve \add statues of\add* lions standing on the six steps—one at each end of each step. \add No throne\add* like that existed in any other kingdom.
\p
\v 20 All of King Solomon’s cups were made of gold, and all the various dishes in ‘The House of the Lebanon Forest’ were refined gold. Nothing was made of silver, because during Solomon’s \add reign\add*, silver wasn’t considered valuable,
\v 21 because the king’s ships went to Tarshish with Huram’s servants. Every three years the fleet would bring back gold, silver, ivory, and apes and baboons,
\v 22 so King Solomon \add became\add* greater than all the other kings in the world, \add both\add* in wealth and in wisdom.
\p
\v 23 Kings from all over the world wanted to come and listen to the wisdom that God had given Solomon.
\v 24 Each of them would bring gifts: gold and silver items, clothes, myrrh and spices, horses and mules. (\add That continued\add* year after year.)
\p
\v 25 Solomon had four thousand stalls for horses and chariots, and twelve thousand horsemen, and he kept \add some\add* in the chariot cities and \add some\add* close by in Jerusalem.
\v 26 He \add ended up\add* ruling over all the kings \add in the region\add*, from the \add Euphrates\add* river \add in the northeast\add*, \add west\add* to the area of the Philistines, and \add south down\add* to the Egyptian border.
\v 27 The king made silver \add as common\add* as stones, and cedar \add timber as common\add* as the sycamore fig trees in the lowlands.
\v 28 Horses were brought in from Egypt for Solomon, and from other countries as well.
\s1 Summary of Solomon’s reign
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Kgs 11:41-43)
\p
\v 29 Everything else that Solomon did, from the beginning to the end, was written down by the prophet Nathan, and in the prophecy by Ahiyah (from Shiloh), and the prophet Ido’s visions about Nevat’s son Jeroboam were also written down.
\v 30 Solomon reigned from Jerusalem over all of Israel for forty years,
\v 31 then he died and was buried with his father in David’s city, and his son Rehoboam replaced him as king.
\c 10
\s1 Israel splits in two
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Kgs 12:1-20)
\p
\v 1 Then Rehoboam went to Shechem, where all of Israel had gathered for his coronation.
\v 2 Meanwhile, Nevat’s son Jeroboam had fled from King Solomon and was \add down\add* in Egypt, but when he heard \add about the coming coronation\add*, he returned from Egypt.
\v 3 So the leaders of the northern tribes summoned Jeroboam, and he went with them to speak to \add Solomon’s son\add* Rehoboam and all of Israel, saying,
\v 4 “Your father made us work very hard, but now, lighten the demands that your father made on us, and we’ll serve you.”
\p
\v 5 “Come back to me in three days.” Rehoboam answered, so the people departed.
\p
\v 6 Then King Rehoboam consulted with the older men who’d advised his father Solomon when he was \add still\add* alive, asking them, “How do you advise me to answer the people?”
\p
\v 7 “If you’re good to those people and do things that please them, and speak kindly to them,” they answered, “then they’ll serve you for a long time to come.”
\p
\v 8 However, Rehoboam rejected their advice, and consulted some of the young men there who’d grown up with him.
\v 9 “How would you guys suggest I answer the people who’ve asked me to lighten the burden on them, compared to my father’s time?”
\p
\v 10 “Those people tell you that your father was hard on them and that you should lighten their load,” his contemporaries advised him. “But you should tell them that your little finger is thicker than your father’s waist,
\v 11 and that whereas he required them to contribute, you’ll be even more demanding. Tell them that he might have whipped them with \add leather\add* whips, but that you’ll whip them with scorpions!”
\p
\v 12 So Jeroboam and all the people returned to \add King\add* Rehoboam on the third day as he’d prearranged,
\v 13 and ignoring the advice of the older men, the king answered the people harshly.
\v 14 He passed on the younger men’s suggestion, telling the people, “I’ll make your burden even heavier. My father rebuked you \add people\add* with whips, but I’ll do it with scorpions.”
\v 15 So the king took no notice of the people’s request, because it was God turning events around, so that what Yahweh had said to Jeroboam (Nevat’s son) through Ahiyah (from Shiloh) would come to pass.
\p
\v 16 When all the Israelites saw that the king hadn’t listened to them, the people \add from the northern tribes\add* answered the king, “What did David have to do with us anyway? We had nothing to gain from Jesse’s son. You might as well all head home, and then they’ll see what happened to David’s dynasty.”
\p So they all went home,
\v 17 and Rehoboam \add ended up\add* only ruling over the Israelites living in the Judah region.
\p
\v 18 King Rehoboam \add (still in Shechem)\add* sent out Hadoram, his supervisor of the forced labourers, but the Israelites hurled stones at him and killed him. \add When he heard about that\add*, King Rehoboam forced his way into his chariot and escaped to Jerusalem,
\v 19 and \add northern\add* Israel has rebelled against David’s descendants to this day.
\c 11
\s1 The scolding of prophet Simayass to Rehoboam
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (1 Kgs 12:21-24)
\p
\v 1 When Rehoboam arrived in Jerusalem, he assembled Judah’s and Benjamin’s warriors: 180,000 chosen to fight against Israel to bring the kingdom back to Rehoboam.
\v 2 But Yahweh spoke to the prophet Shemayah, saying,
\v 3 “\add Go and\add* tell this to Solomon’s son King Rehoboam of Judah, and to all the Israelite people from the Judah and Benjamin tribes:
\v 4 Yahweh says that you mustn’t go and fight with your relatives. Everyone should just go home again, because everything that’s happened came from me.” So they listened to the message from Yahweh, and called off the planned attack against Jeroboam.
\s1 Rehoboam fortifies the cities
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 5 So Rehoboam lived in Jerusalem and fortified the cities throughout Judah:
\v 6 Bethlehem, Eytam, Tekoa,
\v 7 Beyt-Tsur, Sokoh, Adulam,
\v 8 Gath, Mareshah, Zif,
\v 9 Adorayim, Lakish, Azekah,
\v 10 Tsor’ah, Ayalon, and Hebron (all in Judah and Benjamin).
\v 11 He strengthened the fortifications \add in those cities and towns\add*, appointed an army commander, and stocked their storehouses with food and oil and wine.
\v 12 He \add also\add* equipped each of them with shields and spears, and made sure they were well-protected. So Judah and Benjamin were under his control.
\s1 Priests and Levites go down to Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 13 The priests and Levites throughout all of Israel supported Rehoboam from every part of the country.
\v 14 The Levites abandoned their property and their pastureland and moved to Jerusalem and across the rest of Judah, because Jeroboam and his sons stopped them from serving as Yahweh’s priests.
\v 15 Instead Jeroboam established his own priests for his hilltop shrines and for the goat and calf \add idols\add* that he’d made.
\v 16 As well as the priests and Levites, people from all the Israelite tribes who wanted to serve Israel’s god Yahweh came to Jerusalem to sacrifice to Yahweh, their ancestors’ god.
\v 17 So they strengthened the Judah kingdom, and as the people followed David’s and Solomon’s examples, they strengthened Rehoboam’s \add rule\add* for three years.
\s1 Rehoboam’s family
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 18 Rehoboam married Mahalat. She was the daughter of David’s son Yerimot, and her mother was Avihayil, the daughter of Eliav and granddaughter of Jesse.
\v 19 They had \add three\add* sons: Ye’ush, Shemaryah, and Zaham.
\v 20 After her, he married Absalom’s daughter Ma’akah, and they had \add four sons\add*: Abijah, Atay, Ziza, and Shelomit.
\v 21 Rehoboam loved Ma’akah the most out of his eighteen wives and sixty slave-wives. He fathered twenty-eight sons and sixty daughters,
\v 22 but he appointed Ma’akah’s son Abijah to be the leader of his \add older and younger\add* brothers, because that would mark him as the future king.
\v 23 He wisely sent his \add other\add* sons to fortified cities across all the regions in Judah and Benjamin, where he set them up generously and asked the local people to give them wives.
\c 12
\s1 Egypt attacks Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (1 Kgs 14:25-28)
\p
\v 1 However, after Rehoboam had become established and in firm control of his kingdom, he and all the people abandoned Yahweh’s instructions.
\v 2 Because they were unfaithful to Yahweh, King Shishak from Egypt came \add to attack\add* Jerusalem in the fifth year of Rehoboam’s \add reign\add*,
\v 3 with 1,200 chariots and sixty thousand horsemen, along with a huge number of troops, including Egyptians, Libyans, Sukim, and Kushim.
\v 4 He captured Judah’s fortified cities, then moved towards Jerusalem.
\p
\v 5 Then the prophet Shemayah and the leaders of Judah who’d fled to Jerusalem because of Shishak’s \add attacks\add* came to Rehoboam. Shemayah told them, “Yahweh says that it was you \add people\add* who abandoned me, so now I’ve also abandoned you to be defeated by Shishak.”
\p
\v 6 Then the king and the Israelite leaders humbled themselves and said, “Yahweh always does what’s right and fair.”
\p
\v 7 When Yahweh saw that they’d humbled themselves, he gave this message to Shemayah, “They’ve humbled themselves, \add so\add* I won’t destroy them—my rage won’t pour out on Jerusalem through Shishak’s attack, and I’ll give them a way to recover \add in the future\add*.
\v 8 Nevertheless, they’ll become his servants, and they’ll learn \add the difference between\add* serving me versus serving other nations’ kings.
\p
\v 9 When Egypt’s King Shishak invaded Jerusalem, he took the treasures from Yahweh’s temple and the king’s palace. He took everything \add valuable\add*, including the gold shields that Solomon had made.
\v 10 King Rehoboam replaced them with bronze shields, and distributed them to the leaders of the people who guarded the entrance to the king’s palace.
\v 11 After that, whenever the king went to Yahweh’s temple, the guards went with him carrying those bronze shields. Then when the king left again, they’d return them to the guardroom.
\v 12 Because Rehoboam humbled himself, Yahweh stopped being angry with him and didn’t eliminate him, so things were \add reasonably\add* good in Judah.
\p
\v 13 King Rehoboam consolidated his rule in Jerusalem. He reigned for a total of forty-one years—seventeen of which were from Jerusalem city, which Yahweh had chosen from all the tribes in Israel \add as the place\add* to establish his reputation. (Rehoboam’s mother was the Amonit, Na’amah.)
\v 14 Rehoboam did evil \add things\add* because he didn’t make it his priority to get direction from Yahweh.
\p
\v 15 The account of everything Rehoboam did while he was king, including genealogical records, was recorded in the scrolls written by the prophets Shemayah and Ido. Rehoboam and Jeroboam were at war with each other for his entire reign.
\v 16 Then Rehoboam died, he was buried with his ancestors in David’s city, and his son Abijah replaced him as king.
\c 13
\s1 Abijah defeats Jeroboam
\s4 Both kingdoms
\r (1 Kgs 15:1-8)
\p
\v 1 In the eighteenth year of King Jeroboam’s \add reign\add*, Abijah \add started his\add* reign over Judah.
\v 2 He ruled from Jerusalem for three years. (His mother was Uriel’s daughter Mikayah from Giveah.)
\p However, war \add started\add* between Abijah and Jeroboam.
\p
\v 3 Abijah launched the attack with four-hundred thousand strong warriors, and Jeroboam drew up in formation against him with eight-hundred thousand strong warriors in his army.
\p
\v 4 Abijah stood on the top of Mt. Tsemarayim in the Ephraim hill-country and shouted, “Jeroboam and all of Israel, listen to me.
\v 5 Don’t you all understand that Israel’s god Yahweh made a formal agreement that the Israel kingdom was given forever to David and his descendants?
\v 6 But Nevat’s son Jeroboam, who was a servant of David’s son Solomon, took action and rebelled against his master,
\v 7 and a bunch of wicked good-for-nothings gathered around Jeroboam and created a force against Solomon’s son, Rehoboam, when Rehoboam was still an inexperienced, young man and couldn’t stand up to them.
\v 8 So now you \add Israelites\add* are saying that you’ll stand against the kingdom that Yahweh established through David’s descendants. There are certainly many of you, and you have the gold calves that Jeroboam made as gods for you.
\v 9 Didn’t you \add people\add* drive out the Levites, as well as Yahweh’s priests who were Aaron’s descendants? Then you appointed for yourselves ‘priests’ like they have in \add other\add* countries, so that anyone who comes to consecrate himself by bringing a young bull from the herd and seven rams can become a ‘priest’ of your non-gods.
\p
\v 10 “But as for us, Yahweh is our god and we haven’t abandoned him. Our priests who minister to Yahweh are Aaron’s descendants, and the Levites assist them.
\v 11 Every morning and every evening, they sacrifice burnt offerings to Yahweh, and spiced incense. There’s \add sacred\add* bread on the display table, and the lamps in the gold lampstand give light every evening because we’re following our god Yahweh’s instructions, but you \add people\add* have abandoned him.
\v 12 So listen! God is guiding us, and his priests will blast their trumpets to signal battle against you \add people\add*. Listen, you Israelites: don’t fight against Yahweh, your ancestors’ god, because you won’t succeed.”
\p
\v 13 But meanwhile, Jeroboam had sent \add some men\add* around to ambush them from behind, so they were \add both\add* in front of \add the army from\add* Judah and in ambush behind them.
\p
\v 14 When \add the warriors from\add* Judah turned, wow, they realised they were being attacked from in front and from behind. They called out to Yahweh \add for help\add* while the priests blew their trumpets.
\v 15 Then the men from Judah shouted a loud battle cry, and God defeated Jeroboam and all of Israel in front of Abijah and Judah.
\v 16 Israel’s soldiers fled away from Judah’s \add army\add*, and God enabled Judah to defeat them.
\v 17 Abijah and his men thrashed them badly, and \add some\add* five-hundred thousand of their chosen warriors were killed.
\v 18 So the Israelites were subdued at that time, and the people from Judah were victorious because they trusted in Yahweh, their ancestors’ god.
\p
\v 19 Abijah chased after Jeroboam and they captured from Israel the cities of Bethel, Yeshanah, and Efron, and their surrounding villages.
\v 20 Jeroboam never regained military strength during Abijah’s reign, and \add after a time\add* Yahweh struck him \add with sickness\add* and he died.
\p
\v 21 Meanwhile, Abijah became more powerful. He took fourteen wives for himself, and had twenty-two sons and sixteen daughters.
\v 22 The rest of what Abijah did, including his sayings and his behaviour, was written down by the prophet Ido.
\c 14
\s1 King Asa’s rule over Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then Abijah died, and they buried him with his ancestors in David’s city, and his son Asa reigned in his place. During his rule, the country was peaceful for ten years.
\v 2 Asa did what was good and right in his god Yahweh’s eyes—
\v 3 he removed the pagan altars and the hilltop shrines, smashed the sacred \add stone\add* pillars, and cut the Asherah poles down.
\v 4 He ordered \add the people in\add* Judah to worship Yahweh, their ancestors’ god, and to obey his instructions and regulations.
\v 5 He removed all the hilltop shrines from Judah’s cities, and \add also\add* the incense altars, and the kingdom was peaceful under his rule.
\v 6 He fortified Judah’s cities, as the place was quiet without war during those years, because Yahweh had given him peace,
\v 7 and he’d told Judah, “Let’s build these cities with walls around them, and towers, doors, and bars, \add while\add* the land’s quiet around us, because we’ve asked our god Yahweh for guidance. We’ve made our request, and he’s given us rest on all our borders.” So they built and they prospered.
\v 8 \add King\add* Asa had an army of three-hundred thousand men from Judah who carried large shields and spears, as well as 280,000 men from Benjamin who also carried shields, along with bows and arrows. They were all powerful warriors.
\s1 The Kushi invasion repelled
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 9 But then Zerah the Kushi came out against them with an army of a million \add soldiers\add* and three hundred chariots, and he advanced to Mareshah.
\v 10 Asa went out to oppose them, and they deployed for battle in the Tsefat valley near Mareshah.
\v 11 Then \add King\add* Asa called out to his god Yahweh, “Yahweh, it makes no difference to you if there’s one against you, or one million. Help us, Yahweh our god, because we rely on you, and we’ve come against this massive force, trusting in your reputation. Yahweh, you’re our god—don’t let mere humans prevail against you.”
\p
\v 12 So Yahweh helped Asa and the army from Judah to defeat the Kushim, and they fled.
\v 13 Asa and his army chased them \add southwest\add* as far as Gerar. So many Kushim were killed that \add the rest\add* couldn’t save themselves, because they’d been destroyed in front of Yahweh and his army, and the men of Judah carried away a large amount of plunder.
\v 14 They struck all the cities around Gerar, because the Kushim there were terrified of Yahweh. The men from Judah plundered all those cities, because they contained a lot of valuable goods.
\v 15 They also attacked the \add Kushi\add* cattle herdsmen, as well as capturing lots of sheep and camels before returning to Jerusalem.
\c 15
\s1 Azaryah’s prophecy gives courage to Asa
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then God’s spirit came down onto Oded’s son Azaryah,
\v 2 and he went out to \add King\add* Asa and told him, “Listen to me, Asa, and all of Judah and Benjamin: Yahweh is with you \add southerners\add* when you’re with him. And if you look for him, you’ll find him, but if you abandon him, he’ll \add also\add* abandon you.
\v 3 Israel has been without the true God for a long time, as there hasn’t been a priest teaching, so God’s instructions \add have been forgotten\add*.
\v 4 However, they turned to Israel’s god Yahweh when they were in distress, and requested his help, and he helped them.
\p
\v 5 “In those times, it wasn’t safe to travel away, because there was wide unrest among the inhabitants of the \add surrounding\add* regions.
\v 6 Nation crushed nation, and city crushed city, because God \add sent them\add* one trouble after another.
\v 7 But you \add southerners\add*, be strong and don’t give up striving, because your \add hard\add* work will be rewarded.”
\p
\v 8 When \add King\add* Asa heard those words and the prophecy from the prophet Oded’s \add son\add*, he took courage and removed all the detestable \add idols\add* from across Judah and Benjamin, and from the cities he’d captured from the Ephraim hill country. Also, he repaired the altar to Yahweh that \add stood\add* in front of Yahweh’s temple.
\p
\v 9 He assembled all \add the people from\add* Judah and Benjamin, as well as the many people who’d joined them from the Ephraim, Manasseh, and Simeon \add tribes\add*. (Many people had emigrated from Israel \add into Judah\add* when they realised that his god Yahweh was helping him.)
\v 10 They gathered \add together\add* in Jerusalem in June of the fifteenth year of \add King\add* Asa’s reign
\v 11 and sacrificed seven hundred bulls to Yahweh and seven thousand sheep—\add all of which had come\add* from the \add recent\add* plunder.
\v 12 They \add solemnly\add* agreed to sincerely and diligently devote themselves to Yahweh, their ancestors’ god.
\v 13 \add They decided\add* that anyone who didn’t worship Israel’s god Yahweh must be killed, man or woman, regardless of whether or not they had an important position.
\v 14 Together they loudly promised that to Yahweh, then shouted and blew trumpets and horns,
\v 15 and all of Judah celebrated that promise because they were totally sincere and committed. They asked Yahweh to give them guidance, which he did, and he enabled them to have peace throughout their country.
\p
\v 16 King Asa also removed his mother Ma’akah from \add her position as\add* queen because she made a disgusting Asherah \add pole\add*. Asa cut down the pole and crushed it, and burnt it at the Kidron brook,
\v 17 although they didn’t remove the hilltop shrines from Israel. Nevertheless, Asa maintained his singular devotion \add to Yahweh\add* for his whole life,
\v 18 and he brought his father’s sacred things, and his own, into God’s temple—gold and silver items.
\v 19 There was no more war \add in Judah\add* until the thirty-fifth year of Asa’s reign.
\c 16
\s1 Israel comes to invade Judah
\s4 Both kingdoms
\r (1 Kgs 15:17-22)
\p
\v 1 In the thirty-sixth year of \add King\add* Asa’s reign, Israel’s king Baesha invaded Judah and built Ramah to prevent anyone from leaving or entering the land controlled by Judah’s King Asa.
\v 2 Asa took gold and silver from the temple treasuries and the king’s palace, and he sent \add it\add* to King Ben-Hadad of Syria \add modern Aram)\add*, who lived in Damascus, requesting,
\v 3 “\add I want\add* a peace treaty between you and me, and between your family and mine. Listen, I’ve sent gold and silver to you. Go \add and\add* break your agreement with Israel’s King Baesha, so he’ll give up on his attack against me.”
\p
\v 4 Ben-Hadad accepted King Asa’s suggestion and sent his army captains \add to fight\add* against Israel’s cities. They struck Iyon, Dan, and Abel-Mayim, and all the storehouses in the Naphtali cities.
\v 5 When \add King\add* Baesha heard \add about that\add*, he stopped fortifying Ramah and abandoned the project,
\v 6 so King Asa took \add men there\add* from Judah. They carried away all the \add building\add* stones from Ramah, along with the timber which Baesha had been using, and used \add it all\add* to build Geva and Mitspah \add (north of Jerusalem\add* \add )\add*.
\s1 Hanani’s prophecy about coming wars
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 7 At that time, the prophet Hanani went to Judah’s King Asa and told him, “Because you relied on the king of Syria rather than on your god Yahweh, the Aramean king’s army has escaped from being defeated by you.
\v 8 Didn’t the Kushim and the Libyans have a vast number of chariots and horsemen? Yet because you relied on Yahweh, he helped you defeat them,
\v 9 because Yahweh sees everything that’s happening all over the world, and he strengthens the people who trust him completely. You’ve been foolish in what you’ve just done, so from now on, you’ll be facing wars.”
\v 10 But \add King\add* Asa was angry at the prophet, very angry, and had him put in prison, and after that Asa oppressed some of his people.
\s1 The end of Asa’s reign
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (1 Kgs 15:23-24)
\p
\v 11 All of Asa’s projects from the beginning of his reign to the end are written in the scroll about the kings of Judah and Israel.
\v 12 \add King\add* Asa got a disease that started in his feet in the thirty-ninth year of his reign. Even as it got worse, he didn’t try to get Yahweh \add to help him\add*, only doctors.
\v 13 Then Asa died in the forty-first year of his reign,
\v 14 and he was buried in the tomb that he’d had carved out in David’s city. They laid his corpse on a bed of prepared spices and perfumes that had been mixed ready. Then they lit a large bonfire \add in his honour\add*.
\c 17
\s1 Jehoshaphat becomes king
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then Asa’s son Jehoshaphat replaced him as king, and he strengthened their defenses against Israel.
\v 2 He placed troops in all of Judah’s fortified cities, and posted garrisons across Judah and in the cities in Ephraim that his father Asa had captured.
\v 3 Yahweh helped Jehoshaphat because he followed his ancestor David’s example at the beginning, and didn’t ask the Baals for guidance,
\v 4 because he asked his father’s god, and he followed God’s instructions rather than doing the evil things that were done in Israel.
\v 5 So Yahweh enabled him to maintain control of his kingdom, and all of Judah brought tribute to Jehoshaphat, causing him to become very wealthy and highly respected.
\v 6 He was completely devoted to doing what pleased Yahweh, even removing the hilltop shrines and the Asherah \add poles\add* from Judah.
\p
\v 7 In the third year of his reign he sent his officials, Ben-Hayil, Obadiah, Zechariah, Netanel, and Mikayah, to teach in Judah’s cities.
\v 8 They were accompanied by the Levites Shemayah, Netanyah, Zevadyah, Asahel, Shemiramot, Jonathan, Adonijah, Toviyah, and Tov-Adonijah, along with two priests, Elishama and Jehoram.
\v 9 They \add took\add* a scroll with Yahweh’s instructions written on it, and went around Judah’s cities teaching the people.
\s1 Jehoshaphat’s rise
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 10 All the surrounding kingdoms were afraid of Yahweh, so they didn’t attack Jehoshaphat.
\v 11 Some of the Philistines brought tribute to Jehoshaphat, including lots of silver. Also, the Aravim brought him flocks \add totalling\add* 7,700 rams and 7,700 goats.
\v 12 So Jehoshaphat continued to become more powerful, and he built palaces in Judah, as well as storehouses in the cities.
\v 13 He accumulated many supplies in Judah’s cities.
\p He \add also\add* had many powerful warriors in Jerusalem.
\v 14 These are the leaders and numbers from each tribe:
\li1 From the Judah \add tribe\add*, Adnah was the commander, and he had three-hundred thousand warriors,
\li1
\v 15 His assistant Yehohanan commanded 280,000,
\li1
\v 16 Next was Zikri’s son Amasyah, who volunteered for Yahweh and commanded two-hundred thousand warriors.
\li1
\v 17 From the Benjamin \add tribe\add*, Elyada led two-hundred thousand men equipped with bows and shields,
\li1
\v 18 next to him was Yehozavad with 180,000 equipped for war.
\m
\v 19 Those were the \add ones who\add* served the king, along with the \add ones\add* the king had placed in Judah’s fortified cities.
\c 18
\s1 prophet Mikiyas and King Ahab
\s4 Both kingdoms
\r (1 Kgs 22:1-28)
\p
\v 1 \add King\add* Jehoshaphat became very wealthy, and was highly honoured, but then he allied himself by marriage to \add Israel’s\add* \add King\add* Ahab.
\v 2 Some years later, he went up to Ahab in Samaria, and Ahab sacrificed lots of sheep and cattle for him and for the people who were with him, and he incited him to attack Ramot-Gilead—
\v 3 Israel’s King Ahab suggested to Judah’s King Jehoshaphat, “Will you join me to \add attack\add* Ramot-Gilead?”
\p “Wherever you’ll be, I’ll be there,” he responded. “Your people are like my own people, and \add we’re\add* with you in battle.”
\v 4 Then he added, “But we should get Yahweh’s advice first.”
\p
\v 5 So Israel’s king gathered four hundred \add of\add* his prophets and asked them, “Should we attack Ramot-Gilead or not?”
\p “Go ahead,” they answered, “and God will give victory to the king.”
\p
\v 6 But Jehoshaphat asked, “Is there no prophet of Yahweh here that we can ask?”
\p
\v 7 Israel’s king told Jehoshaphat, “\add There’s\add* still one man who would ask Yahweh, but personally I hate him because he never prophesies anything good about me—it’s always something evil. He’s Yimla’s son, Mikayah.”
\p “You shouldn’t talk like that,” said Jehoshaphat.
\p
\v 8 So Israel’s king ordered an official, “\add Get\add* Yimla’s son Mikayah \add here\add* as quick as possible.”
\p
\v 9 Now the kings of Israel and Judah were \add both\add* clothed in their \add royal\add* robes and sitting there on their thrones near Samaria’s entrance gate. They were \add up\add* on a threshing floor, and all the prophets were prophesying in front of them.
\v 10 Kena’anah’s son Zedekiah made himself some iron horns and said, “Yahweh says you’ll use these to gore Syria and destroy them.”
\v 11 All the rest of Ahab’s prophets agreed, saying, “\add Yes,\add* go and attack Ramot-Gilead and you’ll succeed—Yahweh will give the king victory.”
\p
\v 12 The messenger who went to fetch Mikayah warned him, “Listen, the king is pleased with what the other prophets agreed on. Just say the same as them and give a favourable message.”
\p
\v 13 But Mikayah responded, “As sure as Yahweh lives, I’ll only say what my god tells me to say.”
\p
\v 14 When he got to the king, King \add Ahab\add* asked him, “Micah, should we attack Ramot-Gilead for battle, or should I hold back?”
\p “Go ahead and succeed,” he answered. “You’ll be given victory over them.”
\p
\v 15 But the king said to him, “How many times must I keep making you promise that you’ll only say what Yahweh says—not just what I want to hear?”
\v 16 So Mikayah responded, “I saw all of Israel scattered on the hills like sheep without shepherds, and Yahweh said, ‘They don’t have any master, so they’ll all just go home in peace.’ ”
\p
\v 17 Ahab remarked to Jehoshaphat, “Didn’t I tell you that he wouldn’t prophesy anything good about me—only disaster?”
\p
\v 18 But Mikayah continued, “Well, listen to what Yahweh says. I saw Yahweh sitting on his throne, and all the heavenly soldiers standing on each side of him,
\v 19 and Yahweh said, ‘Who’ll entice Israel’s King Ahab so he’ll attack and then die at Ramot-Gilead?’ Some suggested one thing, and others suggested something else.
\v 20 Then a spirit went out and stood in front of Yahweh and said, ‘I myself will entice him.’ ‘How would you do it?’ Yahweh asked him.
\v 21 The spirit replied, ‘I’ll go and inspire all of Ahab’s prophets to tell lies.’ Yahweh said, ‘That’ll work. Go and do it.’
\p
\v 22 “So now, listen. Yahweh placed a deceiving spirit in the mouths of those prophets of yours, and Yahweh has decreed that you’ll face disaster.”
\p
\v 23 Then Kena’anah’s son Zedekiah went up to Mikayah and slapped him hard on the cheek, saying, “\add Yeah?\add* Since when did Yahweh’s spirit leave me and start speaking to you?”
\p
\v 24 “Well, you’ll find out \add soon\add*,” answered Mikayah, “on the day when you go room by room \add in some house\add* to try to hide.”
\p
\v 25 “Arrest Mikayah,” ordered King Ahab, “and return him to Amon the city governor, and to my son Yoash.
\v 26 Tell them that the king’s ordered that this man be put in prison, and be given only strictly-rationed bread and water until I return safely.”
\p
\v 27 “If you return safely,” Mikayah retorted, “then Yahweh mustn’t have spoken to me.”
\s1 The death of Ahab
\s4 Both kingdoms
\r (1 Kgs 22:29-35)
\p
\v 28 So Israel’s King \add Ahab\add* and Judah’s king Jehoshaphat went to Ramot-Gilead.
\v 29 Ahab told Jehoshaphat, “I’ll get changed, \add so I’m not recognised\add*, and go into the battle, but you wear your royal robe.” So Israel’s king disguised himself, and they went into the battle.
\p
\v 30 Meanwhile, Syria’s king had ordered his chariot commanders, “Only attack Israel’s king—don’t bother fighting anyone else.”
\v 31 However, when those chariot commanders saw \add King\add* Jehoshaphat, they mistook him for Israel’s king, and wheeled around to fight him, but Jehoshaphat cried out \add to Yahweh for help\add*, and he helped him—God drew them away from him,
\v 32 because when the chariot commanders realised that it wasn’t Israel’s king, they turned back from \add chasing\add* him.
\v 33 However, one \add Aramean\add* warrior drew his bow without knowing where Ahab was, and struck Israel’s king at a joint in his armour. Ahab ordered the charioteer, “Turn around and get me out of here, because I’ve been \add seriously\add* wounded.”
\v 34 The battle continued for the whole day, and Israel’s king was propped up in his chariot to watch the Arameans, but he died as the sun was setting.
\c 19
\s1 Jehoshaphat gets scolded
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Judah’s king Jehoshaphat returned safely to his home in Jerusalem,
\v 2 but Hanani’s son, the prophet Jehu, confronted him, “Should you have helped the wicked, and befriended those that Yahweh hates? Yahweh’s angry with you because of what you’ve done.
\v 3 However, you have some good points, because you removed the Asherah \add poles\add* from the country, and you’ve been sincere in wanting to follow God.”
\s1 The neneyimu of Hihusapat
\p
\v 4 Jehoshaphat lived in Jerusalem, but one time he went out to the people of Beersheba in the Ephraim hill country and encouraged them back to Yahweh, their ancestors’ god.
\v 5 He appointed judges in Judah’s fortified cities to serve across the country,
\v 6 and he told them, “Take care in what you do, because as judges, you’re not serving people, but serving Yahweh when you make judicial decisions.
\v 7 So revere Yahweh and judge cases carefully, because our god Yahweh doesn’t tolerate injustice, partiality, or bribery.”
\p
\v 8 Jehoshaphat also appointed some Levites and priests in Jerusalem, as well as leaders from some Israelite families, to be judges on Yahweh’s behalf based in Jerusalem,
\v 9 and he instructed them, “You must revere Yahweh and be trustworthy and sincere.
\v 10 When any of your fellow Israelites who live in the cities bring any case to you involving violent crime, or interpreting laws or regulations, and so on, you must caution them so they won’t sin against Yahweh and induce his anger to come against you or them, so do that, and you won’t be guilty.
\v 11 And listen, the chief priest Amaryah is superior to you \add judges\add* in any religious matter, and Ishmael’s son Zevadyah is Judah’s leader for any civil matter concerning the king. Also, you have the Levite officials to assist you \add judges\add*. Act with courage, and let Yahweh assist people who are doing what’s right.”
\c 20
\s1 Jehoshaphat handles a massive invasion
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Sometime after that, the Moabites and Ammonites, along with some of the Meunim,\f + \fr 20:1 \ft Following the Septuagint for this.\f* came to battle against \add King\add* Jehoshaphat.
\v 2 \add Some men\add* came and told Jehoshaphat, “A huge army is coming from Syria beyond the \add Dead\add* sea to attack you, and take note: they’re \add already\add* in Hatsatson-Tamar, also known as Eyn-Gedi.”
\v 3 Jehoshaphat was alarmed and decided to request advice from Yahweh, and he ordered that all of Judah should fast.
\v 4 So Judah assembled to request Yahweh’s help—they came to Jerusalem from all of Judah’s cities to ask Yahweh for help.
\p
\v 5 Jehoshaphat stood in front of all the people from Jerusalem and across Judah, in front of the new temple courtyard,
\v 6 and prayed, “Yahweh, the god of our ancestors, aren’t you the god \add who lives\add* in the heavens? And you rule in all the kingdoms around the world, and you have full power and control—no one can stand against you.
\v 7 Weren’t you our god who drove the previous inhabitants of this land out ahead of your Israelite people? Didn’t you give it to the descendants of Abraham—the one you loved forever?
\v 8 Our ancestors lived in this land and built a temple to honour you, saying,
\v 9 ‘If disaster, \add like\add* war, judgement, epidemic, or famine comes on us, we’ll stand in front of this temple, and in front of you, because your name is honoured here. And we’ll cry to you because of our distress, and you’ll hear and rescue us.’
\p
\v 10 You didn’t allow our Israelite ancestors to enter the countries of Amon, Moab, or Edom when they were travelling from Egypt \add to Canaan\add*. So our ancestors turned away from those areas and didn’t destroy them, but now \add they’re coming here to attack us\add*.
\v 11 Look how they’re repaying us \add for not destroying them\add*—they come \add here\add* to drive us out from the land you gave us as an inheritance.
\v 12 \add So,\add* our god, please punish them, because we’re not strong enough to battle this huge army that’s on its way. We simply don’t know what to do, so we’re looking to you to rescue us.”
\p
\v 13 \add At that time,\add* all of Judah men, women, and children) were standing in front of Yahweh,
\v 14 and Yahweh’s spirit came on Yahaziel. (Yahaziel was the son of Zechariah, son of Benayah, son of Yeiel, son of Matanyah the Levite, and a descendant of Asaf.) In the middle of the assembly
\v 15 he said, “Pay attention, all of Judah, inhabitants of Jerusalem, and King Jehoshaphat. Yahweh has this to say to you all, ‘Don’t be afraid, and don’t be dismayed by this huge force, because it’s not your battle, but God’s.
\v 16 Tomorrow, go down against them. Listen, they’re coming uphill near the Ascent of Tsits, and you’ll find them at the end of the valley, facing the Yeruel wilderness.
\v 17 You won’t \add need\add* to fight them—just take your positions, people from Jerusalem and wider Judah, and watch Yahweh save you. Don’t be afraid or discouraged. Go out there tomorrow and Yahweh will be with you.’ ”
\p
\v 18 Then \add King\add* Jehoshaphat bowed with his face to the ground, and all \add the people from\add* Jerusalem and across Judah fell \add to their knees\add* in front of Yahweh to worship him.
\v 19 Then \add some of\add* the Levites who were descendants of Kohat and Korah stood up to loudly praise Israel’s god Yahweh.
\p
\v 20 They rose early the \add next\add* morning and went out to the Tekoa wilderness, and as they were leaving, Jehoshaphat stood and called out, “Hear me, inhabitants of Jerusalem and Judah. Believe in our god Yahweh, and be confident. Believe his prophets and succeed.”
\v 21 Then he consulted with the people, and he appointed some to sing to Yahweh and some to praise him for his splendour and sinlessness as they went out in front of the \add enemy\add* army, and they were saying, “Give thanks to Yahweh, because his loyal commitment \add endures\add* forever.”
\p
\v 22 As they began to sing and praise Yahweh, he sent ambushers against the Ammonites, Moabites, and Edomites who were attacking Judah, and they were defeated.
\v 23 Then the Ammonites and Moabites turned on the Edomites and completely annihilated them, and after that they slaughtered each other.
\v 24 When Judah’s men arrived at the place overlooking the wilderness, wow, they saw corpses all over the ground. No one had escaped.
\p
\v 25 Then Jehoshaphat and his people went to plunder the spoils, and they found lots of goods among them, as well as clothing,\f + \fr 20:25 \ft The Hebrew ‘bodies/corpses’ seems wrong here.\f* and valuable items. They took that for themselves until there was nothing left to carry away—there was so much plunder that it took three days to collect it all.
\v 26 On the fourth day, they assembled in Berakah valley and praised Yahweh there. That’s why it’s called Berakah valley \add (which means ‘praise/blessing’)\add* to this day.
\v 27 Then all the men from Jerusalem and \add across\add* Judah returned to Jerusalem with \add King\add* Jehoshaphat leading them—celebrating because Yahweh had enabled them to defeat their enemies.
\v 28 When they arrived at Jerusalem, they went to the temple, \add playing\add* harps, lutes, and trumpets.
\v 29 The \add nearby\add* kingdoms were all very afraid of God when they heard that Yahweh had fought against Israel’s enemies,
\v 30 so Jehoshaphat’s kingdom was untroubled, and his god gave him peace from all his neighbours.
\s1 The end of Jehoshaphat’s kingdom
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (1 Kgs 22:41-50)
\p
\v 31 Jehoshaphat was thirty-five years old when he became king, and he reigned over Judah from Jerusalem for twenty-five years. His mother was Shilhi’s daughter, Azuvah.
\v 32 He had similar priorities to his father Asa, doing what pleased Yahweh, and he didn’t deviate from that.
\v 33 \add However,\add* he never removed the hilltop shrines, and the people weren’t really serious about obeying their ancestors’ god.
\p
\v 34 Everything else that Jehoshaphat did, from the beginning to the end \add of his reign\add*, was written down by Hanani’s son Jehu in the scroll about Israel’s kings.
\p
\v 35 Later on, Judah’s King Jehoshaphat made an alliance with Israel’s King Ahazyah (which was a wicked thing to do),
\v 36 and they worked together to make ships at Etsyon-Gever to sail to Tarshish.
\v 37 Then Dodavahu’s son Eliezer from Mareshah prophesied against Jehoshaphat, saying, “When you allied yourself with Ahazyah, Yahweh destroyed what you created.” In due course, the ships were damaged and unable to sail to Tarshish.
\c 21
\p
\v 1 Then Jehoshaphat died and was buried with his ancestors in David’s city, and his son Jehoram replaced him as king.
\s1 Judah’s King Jehoram
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 8:17-24)
\p
\v 2 Jehoram’s \add younger\add* brothers were Azaryah, Yehiel, Zechariah, Azaryah, Michael, and Shefatyah,
\v 3 and their father gave them many gifts of gold and silver and precious things, along with fortified cities in Judah, however he handed the kingdom over to Jehoram because he was the oldest \add son\add*.
\v 4 After Jehoram established firm control of his father’s kingdom, he had all his brothers killed, as well as some of Israel’s leaders.
\p
\v 5 Jehoram was thirty-two years old when he became the king, and he ruled from Jerusalem for eight years.
\v 6 He did \add many of the evil things\add* that Israel’s kings had done, because he married one of \add King\add* Ahab’s daughters and did things that Yahweh had said were evil.
\v 7 Despite that, Yahweh wouldn’t eliminate David’s descendants, because of the promise he’d made with David to ‘keep his lamp burning’ forever.
\p
\v 8 \add Meanwhile, Edom had been\add* under Judah’s control, but during Jehoram’s reign they rebelled and appointed their own king.
\v 9 Jehoram took his commanders and all his chariots, and crossed \add the Jordan\add* \add river\add*, then at nighttime he attacked the Edomites who’d surrounded him and his chariots.\f + \fr 21:9 \ft The meaning of the second half of this sentence is confusing, so other translations might differ.\f*
\v 10 However, Edom \add successfully\add* rebelled against Judah’s control, then Livnah also did the same, because Jehoram had abandoned Yahweh, his ancestors’ god.
\v 11 He also built hilltop shrines around Judah, and he caused Jerusalem’s inhabitants to prostitute themselves,\f + \fr 21:11 \ft This sentence is about worship, not physical adultery, so the reference to prostitution most likely refers to idol worship. Also see verse 13 below.\f* and he drove Judah away \add from Yahweh\add*.
\p
\v 12 Then King Jehoram received a written message from the prophet Elijah: “Your father David’s god Yahweh says that you \add decided\add* not to follow your father Jehoshaphat’s example, or \add good\add* King Asa’s,
\v 13 but rather you imitated the behaviour of Israel’s king, so because of that, you’ve caused Jerusalem’s inhabitants, as well as \add wider\add* Judah, to commit adultery like Ahab’s family did. Also, you’ve killed your own blood brothers—people who were better than you.
\v 14 So listen, now Yahweh is going to severely afflict your people, including your sons and your wives, as well as all your possessions,
\v 15 and you yourself will get an intestinal disease that’ll get worse every day until your innards fall out.”
\p
\v 16 Then Yahweh stirred the Philistines up against Jehoram, as well as the Arabs who lived beside the Kushim,
\v 17 and they attacked Judah. They did a lot of damage and took away everything valuable from the king’s palace. They also took his wives and sons, other than Yehoahaz, his youngest son.
\p
\v 18 After that, Yahweh caused an intestinal disease in Jehoram that couldn’t be cured,
\v 19 and it progressed day after day until, after two years, his intestines fell out, and he died in a lot of pain. His people didn’t make a bonfire in his honour like they’d done for his ancestors.
\p
\v 20 Jehoram was thirty-two when he became king, and he ruled from Jerusalem for eight years. No one regretted it when he died, and his body was buried in David’s city, but not in the royal tombs.
\c 22
\s1 Judah’s king Ahazyah
\s4 Both kingdoms
\r (2 Kgs 8:25-29; 9:21-28)
\p
\v 1 The inhabitants of Jerusalem made his youngest son Ahazyah\f + \fr 22:1 \ft Called Yehoahaz in 21:17 above. (Replace ‘Yeho’ at the front with ‘Yah’ at the end—the meaning ‘Yahweh has held’ remains the same.)\f* king in his place, because all his older brothers had been killed by the Aravi invaders. So Jehoram’s son Ahazyah reigned as Judah’s king.
\v 2 He was twenty-two\f + \fr 22:2 \ft The Hebrew has forty-two here, but we’ve corrected it from 2 Kgs 8:26.\f* when he became king, and he reigned from Jerusalem for one year. His mother was Athaliah, a granddaughter of Israel’s \add King\add* Omri.
\v 3 King Ahazyah behaved just like \add King\add* Ahab’s family had done, because his mother gave him evil advice,
\v 4 so he did what Yahweh had said was evil, just like Ahab’s family, because after his father’s death, they’d become Ahazyah’s advisors, giving destructive suggestions.
\v 5 He followed their advice and joined \add King\add* Jehoram (son of Israel’s King Ahab) to fight against Syria’s King Hazael at Ramot-Gilead, where Jehoram was wounded by the Arameans.
\v 6 Jehoram returned to Jezreel to recover, because he’d been wounded at Ramah in the battle against Aram’s King Hazael. So Judah’s King Ahazyah went up to Jezreel to see Jehoram.
\p
\v 7 God caused Ahazyah’s downfall through his visit to Joram. When Ahazyah arrived, he went with Jehoram to meet Nimshi’s son Jehu, who Yahweh had appointed to kill all of Ahab’s descendants.
\v 8 While Jehu was executing judgement on Ahab’s descendants, he discovered Judah’s officials there, including Ahazyah’s nephews who’d been serving him, and he killed \add all of\add* them \add as well\add*.
\v 9 Then Jehu went searching for Ahazyah and found him hiding in Samaria \add city\add*. They captured him and took him to Jehu, and killed him. They did bury him, because they said, “He was the grandson of \add King\add* Jehoshaphat who tried hard to please Yahweh.” After that, there were no descendants of Ahazyah who were powerful enough to become Judah’s king.
\s1 Judah’s queen Athaliah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 11:1-3)
\p
\v 10 When Ahazyah’s mother Athaliah found out that her son had died, she took action and destroyed all his family members who could become king of Judah.
\v 11 However, a princess named Yehoshav’at took Ahazyah’s son Yoash, and she sneaked him away from among the king’s sons who were about to be killed, and she hid him and his nurse in a bedroom. Yehoshav’at was the daughter of \add the late\add* King Jehoram and the wife of Yehoyada the priest (she was Ahazyah’s sister), \add and she\add* hid Yoash from Athaliah so she couldn’t kill him.
\v 12 He stayed in hiding with them in the temple for six years while Athaliah ruled over Judah.
\c 23
\s1 Revolt against queen Athaliah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 11:4-16)
\p
\v 1 The following year, \add the priest\add* Yehoyada took courage and made an agreement with the commanders of units of a hundred \add soldiers\add*: Azaryah (Jehoram’s son), Ishmael Yehohanan’s son), \add another\add* Azaryah (Obed’s son), Ma’aseyah (Adayah’s son), and Elishafat (Zikri’s son).
\v 2 They went throughout Judah and gathered all the Levites and the clan leaders from all the cities. When they came to Jerusalem,
\v 3 the whole assembly made an agreement with the \add young\add* king in the temple, and Yehoyada told them, “Listen, the \add late\add* king’s son will reign, just as Yahweh said about David’s descendants.
\v 4 \add Now\add* this is what you need to do: one third of you priests and Levites who are on duty on the Rest Day must guard the \add temple\add* entrances.
\v 5 Another third of you must guard the king’s palace, and the \add final\add* third guard the Foundation gate and all the people in the temple courtyard.
\v 6 Don’t let anyone enter Yahweh’s temple other than the priests and the Levites who are on duty, because they’re consecrated. Every one else must respect Yahweh’s instructions \add not to enter\add*.
\v 7 You Levites must be armed and surround the \add young\add* king. You must kill anyone else who tries to enter the temple, but stay close to the king wherever he goes.
\p
\v 8 So the Levites and the other people did everything Yehoyada the priest had instructed. Each \add leader\add* took his men, both those starting duty on the Rest Day, as well as the others finishing their duties that day, because Yehoyada didn’t dismiss any of the groups.
\v 9 Then Yehoyada the priest gave each of those commanders the spears, and the large and small shields which had been put in the temple by King David.
\v 10 He stationed all the people, each one holding their weapon, right across the end of the temple, around the altar and the temple, and all around the king.
\v 11 Then they brought out young Yoash, put a crown on his head, gave him a scroll with the agreement on it, and declared him as king. Then Yehoyada and his sons anointed him \add with olive oil\add* and shouted, “\add Long\add* live the king!”
\p
\v 12 When Athaliah heard the sound of people running, and people praising the king, she went to the temple
\v 13 to look, and wow, the \add young\add* king was standing \add where previous kings had stood\add* by the pillar at the \add temple\add* entrance, with the commanders and trumpeters beside him. The people were all cheering and blowing trumpets, and singers with instruments were leading the praise. Then Athaliah tore her robe and shouted out, “Treason! Treason!”
\p
\v 14 Then Yehoyada the priest instructed the commanders, “\add Kill her,\add* but not \add here\add* at Yahweh’s temple. March her outside and kill anyone who sticks up for her.”
\p
\v 15 They seized her as she reached the Horse gate in front of the palace and killed her there.
\s1 Yehoyada leads back to Yahweh
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 11:17-20)
\p
\v 16 Then Yehoyada \add the priest\add* got the people to formally agree that they and the king would be loyal to Yahweh,
\v 17 so all the people went to Baal’s temple and tore it down. They smashed its altars and idols, and killed Baal’s priest Matan in front of the altars.
\v 18 Then Yehoyada created the roster for the temple \add duties of\add* the priests and Levites that \add King\add* David had \add previously\add* assigned, \add including\add* offering burnt offerings to Yahweh with happiness and singing, following Moses’ written instructions.
\v 19 He also stationed guards at the temple gates so that no one could enter if they were ceremonially ‘unclean’.
\p
\v 20 Then Yehoyada took those commanders, and the leading citizens and leaders, and all the people, and they brought the king out of the temple and down into the palace through the Upper gate, and sat him on the royal throne.
\v 21 Then all of Judah’s people celebrated, and then the city was peaceful and quiet, as Athaliah had been put to death with a sword.
\c 24
\s1 Judah’s king Yoash
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 12:1-16)
\p
\v 1 Yoash was seven years old when he became \add Judah’s\add* king, and he reigned from Jerusalem for forty years. His mother was Tsivyah from Beersheba.
\v 2 Yoash did what pleased Yahweh throughout the lifetime of Yehoyada the priest.
\v 3 Yehoyada procured two wives for him, and they bore him sons and daughters.
\p
\v 4 Sometime later, Yoash decided it would be good to repair Yahweh’s residence,
\v 5 and he gathered the priests and Levites, and told them, “Go out to Judah’s cities and collect enough money across the country to renovate God’s house in the next few years. Make that a priority.” But the Levites didn’t get on to it,
\v 6 so the king summoned Yehoyada and demanded, “Why haven’t you followed up on sending the Levites throughout Jerusalem and \add across\add* Judah to collect the annual tax instituted by Yahweh’s servant Moses for the people to contribute to the costs of the sacred tent?”
\p
\v 7 (The wicked Athaliah’s people had \add previously\add* broken into Yahweh’s residence and \add taken\add* some of the sacred items from Yahweh’s temple to use for Baal \add worship\add*.)
\p
\v 8 The king ordered that a chest be made and placed outside the \add main\add* temple entrance.
\v 9 They made an announcement in Jerusalem and \add throughout\add* Judah that people were to bring to Yahweh the tax that was instituted by God’s servant Moses when they were still in the wilderness.
\v 10 All the officials and the people agreed \add with that policy, so\add* they brought their contributions and dropped them into the chest until it filled up.
\v 11 Every day, when the Levites who’d been given oversight over the chest by the king saw that it was full, they’d fetch one of the king’s secretaries along with one of the head priest’s officials, and they’d empty the box and return it to its place, so they amassed a large sum of money.
\p
\v 12 The king and Yehoyada \add the priest\add* gave the collected funds to the people who worked on the temple renovations. They \add used them\add* to hire stone-workers and carpenters to restore the temple building, as well as for iron and bronze craftsmen.
\v 13 So they started on the work, and the temple restoration went ahead. They rebuilt the temple following its \add original\add* specifications, and strengthened it.
\v 14 Whey they’d finished, they brought the remainder of the funds \add back\add* to the king and Yehoyada, and they made items for the temple, items used in ministry and for burnt offerings, and dishes and gold and silver items. During Yehoyada’s time, they were offering regular burnt sacrifices in Yahweh’s temple.
\p
\v 15 By then, Yehoyada was getting old, and he died at the age of 130,
\v 16 and they buried him with the kings in David’s city, because he’d done \add so much\add* good for Israel, and for God and his temple.
\p
\v 17 After Yehoyada’s death, Judah’s leaders went to \add young King\add* Yoash and declared their loyalty to him, and he listened to them.
\v 18 Then they abandoned the temple of their ancestors’ god Yahweh, and they worshipped the Asherah \add poles\add* and idols, and \add God\add* was angry at Jerusalem and Judah because of their disobedience.
\v 19 He sent prophets to \add tell them\add* to return back to him, and \add although\add* they warned the people, the people didn’t take any notice.
\p
\v 20 Then God’s spirit enveloped Zechariah, the son of Yehoyada the priest, and he stood up and told the people, “God asks why you \add people\add* are violating his commands. Because you abandoned Yahweh, he’s abandoned you \add people\add*.”
\v 21 However, the people conspired against Zechariah, and the king ordered him to be executed by stoning him in the temple courtyard.
\v 22 King Yoash disregarded the loyal commitment that Yehoyada had shown to his father, and killed his son. As Zechariah was dying, he said, “Yahweh will see this and \add find a way to\add* avenge.”
\p
\v 23 Around the end of the year, the Aramean army attacked Judah and Jerusalem, and killed all the people’s leaders. They sent all their plunder \add back to their\add* king in Damascus.
\v 24 Actually, the Arameans only had a smallish army, but Yahweh allowed them to defeat the much larger army \add from Judah\add* and bring punishment to \add King\add* Yoash, because they’d abandoned Yahweh, their ancestors’ god.
\v 25 When they withdrew, they left Yoash with severe wounds, and then his servants conspired against him because he’d killed the son of the priest Yehoyada. So they killed him in his bed, and \add although\add* they buried him in David’s city, he wasn’t buried with the other kings.
\v 26 The ones who’d conspired to kill him were: Zavad (a son of the Ammonite woman Shim’at) and Yehozavad (a son of the Moabite woman Shimrit).
\p
\v 27 The list of King Yoash’s sons, the many prophecies concerning him, and his renovations of the temple, are all written on ‘The Scroll of the Kings’. His son Amatsyah replaced him as king.
\c 25
\s1 Judah’s King Amatsyah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 14:2-6)
\p
\v 1 Amatsyah was twenty-five when he became \add Judah’s\add* king, and he reigned from Jerusalem for twenty-nine years. His mother was Yeho’adin from Jerusalem.
\v 2 He mostly followed Yahweh’s instructions, but not whole-heartedly.
\p
\v 3 Once his rule was established, he killed \add those of\add* his servants who’d assassinated his father \add Yoash\add*.
\v 4 However, he didn’t order their sons to be executed, because he obeyed Yahweh’s instructions that had been written down by Moses, “Parents mustn’t die because of what their children did, and children mustn’t die because of what their parents did, but each individual should be punished for their own disobedience.”
\p
\v 5 Then Amatsyah summoned Judah’s \add men\add* and appointed some, clan by clan, to be leaders of hundreds and of thousands, for all of Judah and Benjamin. They counted three-hundred thousand men who were twenty and over, and suitable for fighting with a spear and shield.
\v 6 He also hired a hundred thousand powerful warriors from Israel for three tonnes of silver.
\p
\v 7 However, a man of God came and told him, “Your majesty, don’t let Israel’s soldiers go with you, because Yahweh isn’t with Israel—those \add people of\add* Israel.\f + \fr 25:7 \ft The northern Israel kingdom also came to be known as ‘Ephraim’ (similar how the area of the Judah and Benjamin tribes (plus others who moved in—see 11:16) became known as ‘Judah’).\f*
\v 8 Even if you go \add and\add* fight courageously in battle, God will cause you to be defeated by the enemy, because God has the power to help and the power to overthrow.”
\p
\v 9 “So what should I do about all the silver that I’ve \add already\add* paid to the troops from Israel?” Amatsyah asked.
\p “Yahweh can do much more for you than that,” God’s man replied.
\p
\v 10 So \add King\add* Amatsyah told the troops from Israel to go home \add again\add*, but they were extremely angry with Judah, and they got home \add still\add* feeling furious.
\p
\v 11 Then Amatsyah took courage and led his army out to the Dead valley, where they slaughtered ten thousand Edomite \add soldiers\add*.
\v 12 Judah rounded up another ten thousand and herded them to the top of a cliff, where they drove them over to kill them.
\p
\v 13 However, \add during that time\add*, the warriors that Amatsyah had sent back \add to Israel\add* raided Judah’s cities \add all the way\add* from Samaria to Beyt-Horon. They’d killed three thousand \add people\add* and taken \add back\add* a large amount of plunder.
\s1 Amatsyah worships false gods
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 14 After \add King\add* Amatsyah returned from fighting the Edomites, he brought \add back\add* their \add idols\add* and set them up to be \add his\add* gods—bowing down to them and offering sacrifices.
\v 15 That made Yahweh very angry at Amatsyah, and he sent a prophet to tell him, “Why did you want those gods who weren’t even able to save their own people from your army?”
\p
\v 16 While he was still speaking, the king snapped at him, “Who asked you to be my counsellor? Stop right this minute or they’ll strike you down!”
\p So the prophet stopped \add talking\add*, but then he added, “I know that God has decided to destroy you because you’ve done that, and because you didn’t listen to my advice.”
\s1 Judah brings trouble on itself
\s4 Both kingdoms
\p
\v 17 Some time later, Judah’s King Amatsyah consulted his advisors, then sent \add a challenge\add* to Israel’s King Yoash (son of Yehoahaz, son of Jehu, “Let’s face each other \add on the battlefield\add*.”
\v 18 But Israel’s King Yoash replied, “A thistle in Lebanon sent \add a message\add* to a cedar in Lebanon, saying, ‘Give your daughter to my son for a wife,’ but some \add random\add* animal passed through the Lebanese countryside and trampled the thistle.
\v 19 You defeated Edom and now it’s gone to your head. So stay in your chair at home and don’t get over-excited, otherwise both you and Judah will be toppled.”
\p
\v 20 But \add King\add* Amatsyah took no notice, because God was planning for him to be defeated for worshipping Edom’s gods.
\v 21 So Israel’s King Yoash advanced, and the two armies faced each other at Beyt-Shemesh in Judah,
\v 22 and Judah was badly defeated by Israel, and all their warriors fled back home.
\v 23 Israel’s King Yoash captured King Amatsyah (son of Yoash, son of Yehoahaz) at Beyt-Shemesh and took him to Jerusalem. Then he tore down Jerusalem’s wall from the Ephraim gate to the Corner gate—a section about 180m long.
\v 24 He took back to Samaria all the gold and silver and other valuable items that Obed-Edom had been guarding in the temple, as well as the valuables from the palace and some hostages.
\p
\v 25 After the death of Israel’s King Yoash (son of Yehoahaz), Judah’s King Amatsyah lived for a further fifteen years.
\v 26 \add The account\add* of everything else Amatsyah did was written in the scroll of ‘The Kings of Judah and Israel’.
\v 27 From the time that Amatsyah had turned from following Yahweh, there was a conspiracy to assassinate him in Jerusalem, but he fled to Lakish. However, they traced him to Lakish and killed him there.
\v 28 They used horses to carry his body back \add to Jerusalem\add*, and he was buried with his ancestors in David’s city.\f + \fr 25:28 \ft The Hebrew actually has the unusual ‘The City of Judah’ here.\f*
\c 26
\s1 Judah’s King Uzziah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 14:21-22; 15:1-7)
\p
\v 1 Then all the people took sixteen-year-old Uzziah and made him king to replace his father Amatsyah.
\v 2 Uzziah restored Eylot \add city\add* to Judah and rebuilt it after his father’s death.
\p
\v 3 Uzziah was sixteen when he became king, and he reigned from Jerusalem for fifty-two years. His mother was Yekaleyah from Jerusalem.
\v 4 \add He\add* did the things that Yahweh said were good, like his father Amatsyah had done.
\v 5 He strived to follow God during the lifetime of Zechariah, who instructed him. During the time that he obeyed Yahweh, God made him successful.
\p
\v 6 Uzziah went to attack the Philistines, and \add successfully\add* broke through the walls at Gath, Yavneh, and Ashdod. He rebuilt Ashdod and other cities in the Philistia \add region\add*.
\v 7 God helped him against the Philistines, against the Aravim who were living in Gur-Baal, and against the Meunim.
\v 8 The Ammonites paid tribute to Uzziah, and his fame spread as far as the Egyptian border because he was becoming more powerful.
\p
\v 9 \add King\add* Uzziah built fortified towers in Jerusalem at the Corner gate, the Valley gate, and at the angle in the wall.
\v 10 He also built towers in the wilderness, and dug a lot of wells, because he had a lot of cattle—both in the lowlands and in the plains. He was also interested in horticulture, so he had workers stationed in his vineyards and in his fertile fields.
\p
\v 11 Uzziah’s army was trained for fighting battles, and organised into divisions set up by Yeiel the scribe and the commander Ma’aseyah, under the supervision of Hananyah, one of the king’s officials.
\v 12 There were 2,600 clan leaders who led the powerful warriors,
\v 13 and \add the full force of\add* 37,500 was under their authority, to support the king against his enemies.
\v 14 Uzziah supplied shields, spears, helmets, armoured vests, bows, and slingshots for the entire army.
\v 15 Using a local invention, he made war machines in Jerusalem to be placed on the towers and corners \add of the walls\add* to fire arrows and large stones. His fame \add now\add* spread widely because he’d received a lot of help that had made him very powerful.
\p
\v 16 However, \add at the peak of\add* his strength, he became very arrogant, and that led to his destruction. He disobeyed his god Yahweh and went into the temple to burn incense on the incense altar.
\v 17 The high priest Azaryah and eighty other brave priests went in after him.
\v 18 They confronted King Uzziah and challenged him, “It’s not \add permitted\add* for you, Uzziah, to sacrifice to Yahweh, only for Aaron’s descendants the priests—the ones consecrated to sacrifice. Leave the sanctuary, because you’ve disobeyed Yahweh God, so now he won’t honour you.”
\p
\v 19 Then Uzziah, who was holding an incense pan, became very angry, but when he \add started\add* raging at the priests, spots of leprosy \add suddenly\add* appeared on his forehead while he was still there beside the incense altar in front of the priests.
\v 20 The high priest Azaryah looked \add more closely\add*, and confirming that it was indeed leprosy on his forehead, hurried him outside. The king was \add now also\add* in a hurry, because Yahweh had afflicted him.
\p
\v 21 King Uzziah had leprosy until he died, so he had to live in an isolated residence and wasn’t allowed to approach the temple. His son Jotham stood in for him—supervising the palace and ruling Judah.
\p
\v 22 The record of all the other things Uzziah did while he was king was written by the prophet Isaiah son of Amots).
\v 23 When Uzziah died, because of his leprosy they buried him with his ancestors in a royal grave in the countryside, and his son Jotham replaced him as king.
\c 27
\s1 Judah’s King Jotham
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 15:32-38)
\p
\v 1 Jotham was twenty-five when he became king, and he reigned from Jerusalem for sixteen years. His mother was Yerusha, daughter of \add the priest\add* Zadok.
\v 2 He did the things that Yahweh said were good like his father Uzziah had done (except that he didn’t trespass in Yahweh’s temple). However, the people were still behaving corruptly.
\p
\v 3 He rebuilt the temple’s Upper gate and extended the Ofel wall.
\v 4 He built cities in the Judah hill country, and built fortresses and towers in the forests.
\v 5 He attacked the Ammonites king and dominated them. Then for the next three years, the Ammonites brought him three tonnes of silver, and 2,000 tonnes each of wheat and barley each year.
\v 6 Jotham became more powerful because he’d established a pattern of obeying his god Yahweh.
\v 7 The record of all the other things Jotham did while he was king, including all his battles, was written on the scroll ‘The kings of Israel and Judah’.
\v 8 He was twenty-five when he became king, and he reigned from Jerusalem for sixteen years.
\v 9 Then \add (at forty-one years old)\add*, Jotham died and was buried in David’s city, and his son Ahaz replaced him as king.
\c 28
\s1 Judah’s King Ahaz
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 16:1-4)
\p
\v 1 Ahaz was twenty-five when he became king, and he reigned from Jerusalem for sixteen years, but he didn’t do what Yahweh wanted, like his ancestor David had.
\v 2 Instead, he followed the example of Israel’s kings, including casting metal idols for the Baal.
\v 3 He offered incense in the Ben-Hinom valley and he burnt his children with fire,\f + \fr 28:3 \ft Probably, but not definitely, referring to the practice of child sacrifice.\f* following the detestable customs of the nations that Yahweh had driven away as the Israelites had entered the region,
\v 4 plus he \add offered\add* sacrifices and burnt incense at the hilltop shrines, and on the hills, and under every large, green tree.
\s1 War between Aram and Israel
\s4 Both kingdoms
\r (2 Kgs 16:5)
\p
\v 5 So his god Yahweh allowed King Ahaz to be defeated by Syria’s king—they attacked and took many prisoners \add back\add* to Damascus. In addition, Yahweh allowed Israel’s king to be victorious, and they slaughtered many fighters.
\v 6 \add Israel’s King\add* Pekah (Remalyah’s son) killed 120,000 powerful warriors in Judah in one day, after they’d abandoned their ancestors’ god.
\v 7 A warrior from Ephraim \add named\add* Zikri killed King Ahaz’s son Ma’aseyah, Azrikam the palace supervisor, and Elkanah the king’s second-in-command.
\v 8 Israel’s soldiers captured two-hundred thousand of their relatives, including their wives and children. They also took a lot of plunder back to Samaria with them.
\s2 The prophet Oded
\p
\v 9 Now there was a prophet of Yahweh named Oded, and he went out to Israel’s army as it was returning to Samaria, and told them, “Listen, your ancestors’ god, Yahweh, was angry with Judah, so he helped you defeat them. However, you \add people\add* killed them in rage, \add and\add* God has taken notice.
\v 10 Now you want to keep the people of Jerusalem and Judah as your own male and female slaves, but that would certainly make you people guilty in your god Yahweh’s view.
\v 11 So then, listen to me and return those captives, \add because\add* they’re your own relatives, and Yahweh is extremely angry at you \add people\add*.”
\p
\v 12 Then some of the leaders of Israel took action and \add confronted\add* those returning from battle: Azaryah (Yehohanan’s son), Berechiah (Meshilemot’s son), Yehizkiyah (Shalum’s son), and Amasa (Hadlay’s son)
\v 13 told the ones returning, “Don’t bring those captives here, because that would be disobedience, and add to the rest of our disobedience and wrongdoing, because \add Yahweh’s already\add* angry at \add us here in\add* Israel.”
\v 14 So the \add returning\add* soldiers released the captives in front of the leaders and all the assembled \add people\add*, and dropped all the plunder \add there as well\add*.
\v 15 Then some men \add from Israel\add* were called out by name to come and search the plunder to find clothes and dress the naked captives \add from Judah\add* and give them sandals. Then they gave them food and drink, as well as oil \add to rub on their wounds\add*. They gave donkeys to those who couldn’t easily walk, and took them to Jericho \add Jericho,\add* the Palm city) which was nearer their relatives, then those men returned to Samaria.
\p
\v 16 Around that time, King Ahaz requested help from the Assyrian kings,
\v 17 because the Edomites had been and attacked Judah and taken captives.
\v 18 Also the Philistines had raided the lowland cities and the Negev, and they’d captured Beyt-Shemesh, Ayalon, and Gederot, as well as Sokoh, Timnah, and Gimzo with their surrounding villages,
\v 19 because Yahweh was humbling Judah because King Ahaz had thrown off restraint there and been very unfaithful to Yahweh.
\v 20 So the Assyrian King Tiglat-Pileser came, but he \add ended up\add* adding to his troubles rather than helping.
\v 21 Ahaz gave the Assyrian king valuables from the temple and the palace and from other leaders, but that didn’t help.
\s1 Ahaz’s disobedience
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 22 While King Ahaz was experiencing those troubles, he disobeyed Yahweh even more
\v 23 and sacrificed to the gods of Damascus because Syria had defeated him). He reasoned, “Since the gods of the Aramean kings helped them, I’ll sacrifice to them, and then they’ll help me.” However, that led to Ahaz’s fall, and to the fall of all of Judah.\f + \fr 28:23 \ft Sometimes in 2 Chronicles, Judah is referred to as Israel, but as that can be confusing for readers, we’ve made adjustments.\f*
\v 24 Then Ahaz gathered all the furnishings \add that were used\add* in the temple, and broke them into pieces and locked the temple doors. Then he set up pagan altars at every Jerusalem intersection,
\v 25 and in every Judah city he set up hilltop shrines to make sacrifices to other gods, thus angering Yahweh, his ancestors’ god.
\p
\v 26 The record of all the other things Ahaz did while he was king was written on the scroll ‘The kings of Judah and Israel’.
\v 27 Then Ahaz died and was buried in David’s city, but not in the tombs of the \add other\add* kings of Israel. Then his son Hezekiah replaced him as king.
\c 29
\s1 Purifying the temple
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 18:1-3)
\p
\v 1 Hezekiah was twenty-five when he became king, and he reigned from Jerusalem for twenty-nine years. His mother was Zechariah’s daughter Abijah.
\v 2 He did what pleased Yahweh, like his ancestor \add King\add* David had done.
\p
\v 3 In the \add very\add* first month of his reign, he unlocked the temple doors and repaired them.
\v 4 Then he summoned the priests and the Levites, assembled them in the eastern temple courtyard,
\v 5 and told them, “\add Now you\add* Levites, listen to me. \add You need to\add* consecrate yourselves now, and consecrate the residence of Yahweh, your ancestors’ god, and remove any defilement from the sacred place,
\v 6 because our ancestors were unfaithful and disobeyed our god Yahweh. Then they abandoned him, lost interest in this temple, and turned their back \add on it all\add*.
\v 7 They extinguished the lamps and locked the temple up. \add After that\add*, they didn’t burn any incense or offer any burnt sacrifices in the sacred place of Israel’s god.
\v 8 \add That’s why\add* Yahweh was angry at Jerusalem and \add all of\add* Judah, and allowed us to become a place of terror and horror and scorn, as you’ve all seen with your \add own\add* eyes.
\v 9 As a result, our fathers died in battle, and our wives and children have been captured and taken to other countries.
\p
\v 10 Now I sincerely want to make an agreement with Israel’s god Yahweh, so that his fierce anger will turn away from us.
\v 11 So lads, don’t mess around, because Yahweh has chosen you to stand in his presence to serve him, and to be ministering and burning incense.
\p
\v 12 Then \add these\add* Levites took action:
\li1 from the Kohatites: Mahat (Amasay’s son) and Joel Azaryah’s son),
\li1 from the descendants of Merari: Kish (Avdi’s son) and Azaryah (Yehalel’el’s son),
\li1 from the Gershonim: Yoah (Zimah’s son) and Eden (Yoah’s son),
\li1
\v 13 from Elitsafan’s descendants: Shimri and Yeiel,
\li1 from Asaf’s descendants: Zechariah and Matanyah,
\li1
\v 14 from Heyman’s descendants: Yehiel and Shimei, and
\li1 from Yedutun’s descendants: Shemayah and Uziel.
\p
\v 15 They assembled their relatives and consecrated themselves, then they entered the temple to purify it, as the king had ordered as a result of Yahweh’s message.
\v 16 The priests entered the inner part of the temple to purify it, and they took everything they found that shouldn’t be in there and brought it out to the temple courtyard, and then the Levites took it \add all\add* out \add to be burnt, down in\add* the Kidron valley.
\p
\v 17 They began the purification at the beginning of March and worked outwards, reaching the porch by the eighth, and then it took eight more days \add for the courtyard\add*, so they finished on the sixteenth.
\p
\v 18 Then they reported to King Hezekiah, “We’ve purified the whole of Yahweh’s temple, \add including\add* the altar for burnt offerings and all its utensils, and the bread display table and all its utensils.
\v 19 We’ve prepared and consecrated all the items which King Ahaz rejected during his unfaithful reign, and they’re \add back\add* in front of Yahweh’s altar.”
\s2 Temple sacrifices restored
\p
\v 20 Early the next morning, King Hezekiah assembled the city officials and went to Yahweh’s residence,
\v 21 taking seven bulls, seven rams, seven male lambs, and seven male goats to be a sin offering for the kingdom, the sanctuary, and Judah. He instructed the priests (Aaron’s descendants) to sacrifice the animals to Yahweh on the altar.
\v 22 So they slaughtered the bulls and took the blood and sprinkled it on the altar. Then they did the same for the rams, and then the lambs.
\v 23 Finally, they brought the goats for the sin offering to the front, and the king and the people placed their hands on them,
\v 24 and then the priests slaughtered them and splashed their blood on the altar so Yahweh would forgive all of Israel’s disobedience. (The king had ordered the burnt offerings and the sin offering be for all of Israel.)
\p
\v 25 Then he told the Levites \add to stand\add* in the temple with cymbals, harps, and lyres—obeying what David and his prophets Gad and Nathan had commanded. (Yahweh had \add actually\add* given those orders through his prophets.)
\v 26 The Levites stood with David’s instruments, and the priests with their trumpets,
\v 27 then Hezekiah said to offer the burnt offering on the altar. When they started to slaughter the animals, \add the people\add* sang and praised Yahweh as the trumpets were blown and the other instruments played.
\v 28 The entire assembly were bowing down \add and worshipping\add* as the singers sang and the trumpeters played, until the burnt offering was completed,
\v 29 then the king and everyone with him bowed down and worshipped.
\v 30 Then King Hezekiah and his officials ordered the Levites to sing praises to Yahweh using compositions by David and the prophet Asaf, so they cheerfully sang praises and bowed down and worshipped.
\p
\v 31 Then Hezekiah responded, “You’ve consecrated yourselves to Yahweh, \add so\add* come near and bring your sacrifices and thanksgiving \add gifts\add* into Yahweh’s house.” So the assembly brought in their sacrifices and thanksgiving \add gifts\add*, plus those who wanted to brought their sacrifices to be burnt.
\p
\v 32 Altogether they brought seventy bulls, a hundred rams, and two hundred lambs to be completely burnt on the altar,
\v 33 as well as six hundred bulls and three thousand sheep as dedicated offerings.
\v 34 There weren’t enough priests to skin all the burnt offerings, so their relatives the Levites helped them until the work was finished and until all the priests had consecrated themselves, because the Levites had worked quicker to consecrate themselves than the priests had.
\v 35 In addition to the burnt offerings, there was the fat from the peace offerings, and there were drink offerings.
\p So the service in Yahweh’s temple was reinstituted,
\v 36 and Hezekiah and all the people celebrated about what God had prepared for the people, because it had all happened fairly quickly.
\c 30
\s1 Hezekiah orders a pass-over celebration
\s4 Both kingdoms
\p
\v 1 Then \add King\add* Hezekiah sent \add invitations\add* to all of Israel and Judah including Ephraim and Manasseh to come to Yahweh’s \add temple\add*,
\v 2 because the king had consulted with his officials and the whole assembly in Jerusalem \add and decided\add* to \add have a late\add* celebration of the Passover in April.
\v 3 (They couldn’t have done it at the \add proper\add* time because the priests hadn’t consecrated themselves sufficiently then, and so the people hadn’t gathered in Jerusalem.)
\v 4 The plan pleased the king and all the people,
\v 5 so they sent messages across all of Israel \add and Judah\add*, from Beersheba \add in the south\add* to Dan \add in the far north\add*, for \add the people\add* to come to Jerusalem to celebrate the Passover \add to honour\add* Israel’s god Yahweh. (They hadn’t previously been observing the written instructions.)
\v 6 Runners took the letters from the king and his officials, \add going\add* to all of Israel and Judah with the king’s command: “Descendants of Israel, return to Yahweh, the god of Abraham, Isaac, and Israel, and he’ll return to you—the group that escaped from the control of the Assyrian kings.
\v 7 Don’t be like your fathers and brothers who weren’t faithful to Yahweh, their ancestors’ god, \add provoking him\add* to leave them decimated, as you can see.
\v 8 So don’t be stubborn like your ancestors were. Obey Yahweh and come to the sanctuary that he’s consecrated forever, and serve your god Yahweh so he’ll turn his anger away from you.
\v 9 If you \add descendants of Israel\add* return to Yahweh, your brothers and sons will be shown mercy by their captors and \add be able to\add* return to this land, because your god Yahweh is gracious and compassionate, and he won’t ignore you if you’ll turn back to him.
\p
\v 10 The messengers went from city to city in the Ephraim and Manasseh \add regions\add*, as far \add north\add* as Zebulun, but many people there laughed at them and mocked them.
\v 11 However, some of the people from Asher, Manasseh, and Zebulun humbled themselves and went to Jerusalem.
\v 12 Also in Judah, God moved the people to want to obey the king and his officials and Yahweh’s instructions.
\s1 The pass-over celebration
\s4 Both kingdoms
\p
\v 13 The next month, a huge crowd gathered in Jerusalem to celebrate the Festival of Flat Bread.
\v 14 They took action and removed the altars \add to Baal\add* in Jerusalem, and all the incense altars, and threw them down into the Kidron valley \add to be burnt there\add*.
\v 15 In the middle of the month, they slaughtered the Passover lambs. The priests and Levites had been ashamed, so they’d consecrated themselves, then they brought the burnt offerings into the temple.
\v 16 They stood at their stations, following the instructions written by Moses, then the Levites handed the \add bowls of\add* blood to the priests to sprinkle \add on the altar\add*.
\v 17 Because many people in the assembly hadn’t consecrated themselves, the Levites \add assisted them\add* by slaughtering the lambs and consecrating them to Yahweh.
\v 18 Although most of the people \add who’d travelled down\add* from Ephraim, Manasseh, Issachar, and Zebulun hadn’t purified themselves, they ate the Passover \add food\add* anyway despite the written \add instructions\add*, because Hezekiah had prayed to Yahweh for them, “May Yahweh, who is good, declare as innocent
\v 19 everyone who sincerely wants to honour and obey Yahweh, their ancestors’ god, even if they haven’t completed the formal purification ceremonies for the temple.”
\v 20 Yahweh took notice of Hezekiah’s \add request\add*, and forgave the people,
\v 21 so the Israelites who’d come to Jerusalem celebrated the Festival of Flat Bread for seven days with great excitement. The Levites and priests enthusiastically praised Yahweh every day using their musical instruments.
\v 22 Hezekiah sincerely thanked all the Levites who were very insightful about worshipping Yahweh. Then \add everyone\add* ate for the prescribed seven days—sacrificing peace offerings and confessing to Yahweh, their ancestors’ god.
\s1 The pass-over celebration gets extended
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 23 Then the whole group decided to celebrate for seven \add more\add* days, so they enthusiastically celebrated for another whole week,
\v 24 because Judah’s King Hezekiah had provided a thousand bulls and seven thousand sheep for the people, and the officials had also contributed another thousand bulls and ten thousand sheep. Many \add more\add* priests \add also\add* consecrated themselves.
\v 25 All the people from Judah celebrated, including the priests and Levites, and everyone who’d come \add down\add* from Israel to stay in Judah (whether just for the occasion, or to live permanently), plus \add others\add* living in Judah.
\v 26 Everyone in Jerusalem was very happy, because nothing like that had happened in Jerusalem since the time when David’s son Solomon was king.
\v 27 Finally, the priests and Levites stood up and prayed a blessing on the people, and their voice was heard because their prayer reached God’s holy residence in the heavens.
\c 31
\s1 Hezekiah’s religious reforms
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 When that was all over, all \add those\add* Israelites went out to Judah’s cities and shattered the pillars and cut down the Asherah poles, and they tore down the hilltop shrines and altars from the entire Judah-Benjamin \add region\add*, and also up in the Ephraim-Manasseh \add region\add*. Once they’d finished that, they all returned to their own land and cities.
\p
\v 2 \add King\add* Hezekiah allocated the priests and Levites \add into\add* divisions for the various different types of duties: for doing burnt offerings and peace offerings, for other services, and for thanksgiving and praise at the temple gates.
\v 3 The king contributed some of his own funds for burnt offerings \add to be sacrificed\add* each morning and evening, as well as for the Rest Days and the New Moon celebrations, and on the other special days as per the written instructions that had come from Yahweh.
\p
\v 4 He told the people living in Jerusalem to give the \add prescribed\add* portions to the priests and Levites, so that they could focus on serving Yahweh,
\v 5 and as soon as the message reached them, the Israelites \add started\add* giving the first portions of \add their harvests of\add* grain, new wine, oil, and honey, and of all the produce from the countryside, so they brought a tenth of \add the harvests of\add* all their crops.
\v 6 The people from Israel and Judah who were living in Judah’s cities also brought a tenth of their cattle and sheep, as well as a sacred tenth consecrated to their god Yahweh, and it was all put in big piles.
\v 7 That all began in May and was finished by September,
\v 8 and \add when King\add* Hezekiah and his officials came and saw the piles, they praised Yahweh and his Israelite people.
\v 9 When Hezekiah asked the priests and Levites about the piles,
\v 10 Azaryah the chief priest (a descendant of Zadok) told him, “Since the people started bringing their contributions to the temple, we’ve been eating well, and even had left-overs, because Yahweh has blessed his people, and all this is the remainder.”
\p
\v 11 So then Hezekiah ordered them to prepare the temple storerooms, which they did,
\v 12 and they brought in the contributions and the tenths \add that had been given\add*, and the things faithfully dedicated \add to Yahweh\add*. A Levite, Konaniyah, was appointed as supervisor, with his brother Shimei as deputy.
\v 13 Those two supervised Yehiel, Azazyah, Nahat, Asahel, Yerimot, Yozavad, Eliel, Yismakyah, Mahat, and Benayah, by the arrangement of King Hezekiah and Azaryah the temple supervisor.
\p
\v 14 The Levite Yimnah’s son, Kore, who guarded the temple’s eastern gate, was put in charge of the free-will offering to God—to distribute those offerings that were made to Yahweh, as well as any consecrated items.
\v 15 Eden, Minyamin, Yeshua, Shemayah, Amaryah, and Shekanyah faithfully assisted him in the priests’ cities to distribute those things to the \add different\add* divisions of priests, regardless of their status or size.
\v 16 Every male who was at least three years old, and was listed in the genealogical records, received a portion for their service in the temple, as each division performed their assigned duties.
\v 17 Each priestly clan kept genealogical records \add and received a portion\add*, and so did the Levites who were at least twenty years old, all according to their divisions,
\v 18 as well as their wives and young children, and sons and daughters whose names were on the genealogies, because they’d also faithfully consecrated themselves for the sacred work.
\v 19 Also, there were people chosen by name to give a portion to each male among Aaron’s descendants, the priests, who had pasture land around their cities, as well as to each Levite included on the genealogical records.
\p
\v 20 So \add King\add* Hezekiah did that throughout Judah, and he continued to faithfully follow his god Yahweh’s instructions.
\v 21 He tried hard to be diligent and sincere in whatever he did for the temple operation, as he followed God’s rules and commands, and he prospered.
\c 32
\s1 The Assyrians threaten Jerusalem
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 18:13-37; 19:14-19,35-37; Isa 36:1-22; 37:8-38)
\p
\v 1 After all those acts of faithfulness \add by Hezekiah\add*, the Assyrian King Sennacherib invaded Judah. He besieged the fortified cities and prepared to capture them for himself.
\v 2 When \add King\add* Hezekiah saw that Sennacherib had entered \add the country\add* and \add clearly\add* intended to attack Jerusalem,
\v 3 he consulted with his officials and army leaders about blocking up the springs outside the city, and they helped him.
\v 4 Many people gathered and blocked the springs from feeding the stream that flowed through the area, saying, “Why should the Assyrian kings come and find plenty of water?”
\v 5 Then he strengthened his fortifications, repairing broken \add sections of the\add* wall, and built towers and an outer wall. He strengthened the supporting wall \add on the east side of\add* David’s city. He \add also\add* manufactured many weapons and shields,
\v 6 and he appointed army commanders and assembled them at the plaza by the city gate, and encouraged them with these words,
\v 7 “Be strong and be courageous. Don’t be scared or discouraged because of the Assyrian king, or by his massive army, because we have someone on our side who’s more powerful than him.
\v 8 They can only rely on human strength, but we have our god Yahweh to help us and fight our battles \add for us\add*.” So the people took strength from King Hezekiah’s encouragement.
\p
\v 9 Later, when the Assyrian King Sennacherib was surrounding Lakish \add city\add* with his army, he sent some servants to Jerusalem, to Judah’s King Hezekiah and all the residents, saying,
\v 10 “I,Sennacherib, the king of Assyria, am asking what you’re all trusting in as you’re staying there in Jerusalem while it’s besieged.
\v 11 Hezekiah tells you, ‘Our god Yahweh will rescue us from the Assyrian king,’ but he’s misleading you, and you’ll all die of hunger and thirst!
\v 12 Wasn’t it Hezekiah who removed his hilltop shrines and his altars, and spoke to \add the people of\add* Jerusalem and \add all of\add* Judah, telling you to bow down at only one altar and offer sacrifices on it?
\v 13 Don’t you people know what my ancestors and I have done to the people groups from other countries? Were any of their gods able to save them from me?
\v 14 Which one of those nations’ gods which my ancestors destroyed is a precedent showing that your god can rescue anyone from me?
\v 15 So don’t let Hezekiah deceive you or mislead you like this. Don’t believe him, because no god of any nation or kingdom has been able to save his people from my ancestors or from me. So your god certainly won’t be able to protect you from my army.
\p
\v 16 Then Sennacherib’s servants said \add even\add* more against the god Yahweh, and against his servant Hezekiah,
\v 17 plus he wrote letters insulting Israel’s god Yahweh and speaking against him, writing, “Just like the \add other\add* nations’ gods couldn’t save their people from me, so too Hezekiah’s god won’t be able to protect his people from my strength.”
\v 18 Then the Assyrians shouted loud \add threats\add* in Hebrew to the people of Jerusalem who were on the wall, to \add try to\add* frighten and terrify them so they could capture the city—
\v 19 telling them Jerusalem’s god was just another god made by people, and just as useless as other countries’ gods.
\p
\v 20 Then King Hezekiah and Isaiah son of the prophet Amots) called out to the heavens \add requesting God’s help\add*,
\v 21 and Yahweh sent a messenger who destroyed the powerful warriors, and the leaders and chiefs in the Assyrian army, and then their king had to return home in shame. When he went into the temple of his god, some of his sons ran him through with a sword.
\p
\v 22 So that’s how Yahweh saved Hezekiah and Jerusalem’s inhabitants from the Assyrian King Sennacherib, and from the power of other nations, and he gave them peace on all sides.
\v 23 Many people came to Jerusalem to bring offerings for Yahweh and valuable gifts for Judah’s King Hezekiah, and after all that, he was highly respected by the other nations.
\s1 Hezekiah’s illness
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 20:1-3,12-19; Isa. 38:1-3; 39:1-8)
\p
\v 24 Some time afterwards, \add King\add* Hezekiah became very sick and was expected to die, but he prayed to Yahweh, and Yahweh answered him with a miracle.
\v 25 However, Hezekiah was very proud and didn’t thank Yahweh, so his anger fell on Jerusalem and Judah.
\v 26 Then Hezekiah and Jerusalem’s inhabitants humbled themselves, and so Yahweh’s anger didn’t affect them during Hezekiah’s lifetime.
\s1 Hezekiah’s wealth
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 27 \add King\add* Hezekiah had become very wealthy, and he was highly respected. \add He established\add* treasuries for gold and silver, for gemstones and spices, and for shields and other valuable things,
\v 28 as well as storehouses for grain, wine, and \add olive\add* oil, and stalls for his flocks and all kinds of livestock.
\v 29 He built cities for himself, and acquired large numbers of sheep and goats, and also cattle, because God had made him very prosperous.
\p
\v 30 Hezekiah blocked off the upper outlet at the Gihon spring and directed the water down the western side of David’s city. All his projects were successful,
\v 31 and so when the Babylonian leaders sent messengers to ask about the miracle that had been done in the country, God left him \add alone\add* to test him and see what he really believed.
\s1 Hezekiah’s reign ends
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 20:20-21)
\p
\v 32 The record of all the other things Hezekiah did while he was king, which includes a record of his loyal commitment, was written on the scroll ‘The kings of Judah and Israel’.
\v 33 Then Hezekiah died and was buried in the upper tombs with David’s other descendants, and everyone in Jerusalem and across Judah honoured him. Then his son Manasseh replaced him as king.
\c 33
\s1 Judah’s king Manasseh
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 21:1-9)
\p
\v 1 Manasseh was twelve when he became king, and he reigned from Jerusalem for fifty-five years.
\v 2 He did was what Yahweh had said was evil, like the disgusting things of the people groups that Yahweh had driven out ahead of the Israelites.
\v 3 The hilltop shrines that his father Hezekiah had torn down, he turned around and rebuilt them and erected altars to the Baals, and he made Asherah \add poles\add*, and he bowed down to all the stars in the sky and served them.
\v 4 He built altars in Yahweh’s temple in Jerusalem that Yahweh had previously said that his name would always be associated with.
\v 5 He built altars for all the stars in the sky in both of the temple courtyards,
\v 6 and he made his sons pass through the fire in the Ben-Hinom valley, and he practiced fortune-telling and sorcery and magic, and connected with mediums and spirits. He did so much evil in Yahweh’s eyes that it provoked him into getting angry.
\p
\v 7 He made an idol and put it in the temple, \add yes,\add* where God had told David and his son Solomon, “My name will be associated with that building forever, there in Jerusalem, which was chosen from among all of Israel’s tribes.
\v 8 If Israel will be careful to do everything that I’ve instructed them and follow all the regulations and statutes that were given to them through Moses, then I won’t force them to leave the land again that I gave to their ancestors.”
\v 9 So Manasseh led Jerusalem’s inhabitants astray, and \add all of\add* Judah—doing more evil than the nations that Yahweh had destroyed ahead of the Israelites.
\p
\v 10 Then Yahweh spoke to \add King\add* Manasseh and his people, but they paid no attention,
\v 11 so Yahweh brought in the Assyrian king’s army leaders and they captured Manasseh. They put a hook \add in his nose\add* and bronze chains around his feet, and they took him away to Babylon.
\v 12 When he was there suffering, he humbled himself in the presence of his ancestors’ god Yahweh, and pleaded with him \add for help\add*.
\v 13 When he prayed, Yahweh listened to him and answered his request and brought him back to Jerusalem to his kingdom. Then Manasseh realised that Yahweh \add really\add* is God.
\p
\v 14 After that, he rebuilt the outer wall for David’s city on the west of Gihon to the Fish gate and around the hill—also making it higher. Then he stationed army commanders in all of Judah’s fortified cities.
\v 15 He removed the foreign gods and idols from the temple, as well as all the altars that he’d built on the temple mount and throughout Jerusalem, and he dumped them \add all\add* outside the city.
\v 16 Then he rebuilt Yahweh’s altar and sacrificed peace offerings and thank offerings on it, and he told Judah to serve Israel’s god Yahweh.
\v 17 However, the people continued to offer sacrifices at the hilltop shrines, but \add now\add* only to their god Yahweh.
\p
\v 18 The record of all the other things Manasseh did while he was king, including his prayer to his god and the messages spoken to him by the prophets, was written on the scroll ‘The kings of Israel’.
\v 19 It’s written in ‘The Words of Hozay’ about his disobedience and unfaithful activities, and how he built the hilltop shrines and Asherah \add poles\add* and idols before he humbled himself, as well as the response to his pleading in his prayer.
\v 20 Then Manasseh died and was buried at his palace, and his son Amon replaced him as king.
\s1 Judah’s King Amon
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 21:19-26)
\p
\v 21 Amon was twenty-two when he became king, and he ruled from Jerusalem for two years.
\v 22 He did what Yahweh had said was evil, just like his father Manasseh had done, and he sacrificed to all the idols his father had made, and served them.
\v 23 He didn’t humble himself and turn to Yahweh like his father had, so Amon’s guilt was even worse.
\p
\v 24 Then Amon’s servants conspired against him, and assassinated him in his palace,
\v 25 but the local people executed everyone who’d conspired against King Amon, and made his son Josiah king in his place.
\c 34
\s1 Judah’s King Josiah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 22:1-2)
\p
\v 1 Josiah was eight when he became king, and he reigned from Jerusalem for thirty-one years.
\v 2 He did what Yahweh had said was right, and made similar decisions to his ancestor David—not deviating to the right or the left.
\p
\v 3 In the eighth year of his reign, while he was still a teenager, he began to learn more about the god of his ancestor David, and in the twelfth year, he began to remove the hilltop shrines from Jerusalem and \add across\add* Judah, as well as the Asherah poles, and the carved and cast metal idols.
\v 4 He watched while they tore down the altars for the Baals and cut down the incense altars that were above them. He shattered the Asherah poles and the idols, then crushed them \add to powder\add*, which he scattered over the graves of the people who’d sacrificed to them.
\v 5 He burnt the bones of \add those\add* priests on their altars, and \add that’s how\add* he purified Jerusalem and \add all of\add* Judah,
\v 6 as well as the cities in \add the tribes of\add* Manasseh, Ephraim, Simeon, and \add as far north as\add* Naphtali, including in the ruins around them.
\v 7 He tore down the altars and the Asherah poles, ground the idols into pieces, and cut down the incense altars in all of Israel, before returning to Jerusalem.
\p
\v 8 In his eighteenth year as king, in order to purify the land and the temple, he sent Atsalyah’s son Shafan, Ma’aseyah the city official, and Yo’ahaz’s son Yoah the secretary, to repair his god Yahweh’s residence.
\v 9 They went to Hilkiyah the high priest and handed over the funds that had been donated for God’s house, which had been collected by the Levites as doorkeepers—\add the money\add* was from \add the tribes of\add* Manasseh, Ephraim, and other places in \add the northern\add* Israel \add kingdom\add*, as well as from Jerusalem’s inhabitants and \add people across\add* all of Judah and Benjamin.
\v 10 The priests then paid the workers who’d been assigned to \add work in\add* Yahweh’s temple to repair and strengthen it.
\v 11 They also paid the carpenters and builders to buy cut stone and timber for the beams and braces which Judah’s kings had allowed to deteriorate,
\v 12 and the workers did their work faithfully. Their supervisors were Yahat and Obadiah Levites descended from Merari), as well as Zechariah and Meshulam (descendants of \add Levi’s son\add* Kohat), and all the Levites who were good on their musical instruments.
\v 13 They \add also\add* supervised the porters and people working on various \add other\add* tasks. Some of the Levites were secretaries, officials, and guards.
\p
\v 14 When they were bringing the money out of the temple, Hilkiyah the \add high\add* priest found a scroll containing Yahweh’s instructions that had been given via Moses.
\v 15 Hilkiyah informed the scribe Shafan that he’d found a Torah scroll in the temple, and he gave him the scroll.
\v 16 Shafan took the scroll \add with him\add* to the king and reported to him, “Your servants are doing everything they were assigned to do.
\v 17 They’ve taken the cash that was in Yahweh’s residence, and passed it on to the supervisors of the workers.”
\v 18 Then Shafan told the king, “\add I’ve brought\add* a scroll that the priest Hilkiyah gave me,” and then he started reading it out to the king.
\p
\v 19 When the king heard the words of God’s instructions, he tore his clothes \add in grief\add*.
\v 20 Then he commanded Hilkiyah, Shafan’s son Ahikam, Micah’s son Avdon, Shafan \add himself\add*, and the king’s \add special\add* advisor Asayah,
\v 21 “Go \add and\add* ask Yahweh on my behalf, and on behalf of everyone in Israel and Judah, what he wants us to do with respect to the instructions on the scroll that’s \add just\add* been found, because \add it seems that\add* Yahweh will be very angry at us because our ancestors haven’t followed all of Yahweh’s instructions as they’re written on that scroll.”
\p
\v 22 So Hilkiyah and the others went to Jerusalem’s Second district, to the prophetess Huldah (wife of Shalum, son of Tokahat, son of Hasrah who took care of the temple robes). They passed on the king’s question,
\v 23 and she replied, “Israel’s god Yahweh says to tell the king:
\v 24 Yahweh says, ‘Listen, I’m going to bring disaster on this place and its inhabitants, as per all the curses written on that scroll that was read to Judah’s king,
\v 25 because they’ve abandoned me and offered incense to other gods. \add That’s\add* made me angry, and \add so\add* my rage will be poured out on this place, and it can’t be prevented.’
\v 26 However, Judah’s king sent you all to ask for Yahweh’s direction, so tell him, ‘Israel’s god Yahweh says that \add after\add* hearing \add the scroll’s\add* words,
\v 27 you were sincere, and you humbled yourself in front of God when you heard his words about this place and its inhabitants. \add Yes,\add* Yahweh declares you’ve humbled yourself in front of me and torn your clothes, and wept in front of me, and I’ve personally heard you.
\v 28 So listen, I’ll allow you to die and be buried in peace, and \add so\add* your eyes won’t see the disaster that I’ll send to this place and its inhabitants.’ ”
\m So they took that response back to the king.
\p
\v 29 Then the king summoned all the elders from Jerusalem and from across Judah,
\v 30 and they all walked up to the temple, with the inhabitants of Jerusalem and \add all of\add* Judah, and the priests and the Levites, and all the people, irrespective of their standing in the community. Then the king read everything out to them that was on the Torah scroll that had been found in the temple.
\v 31 Then the king stood at his \add normal\add* place and made an agreement with Yahweh: to obey Yahweh and follow his instructions and his regulations and statutes, sincerely and with all his energy—doing what was in the joint understanding that had been written on the scroll.
\v 32 Then he had everyone from Jerusalem and Benjamin take a stand \add in agreement to it\add*, and \add so\add* Jerusalem’s inhabitants renewed their agreement with their ancestors’ god.
\v 33 So \add King\add* Josiah removed all the disgusting \add idols\add* from all the Israelite regions, and he made everyone in Israel serve their god Yahweh—during his lifetime they never turned away from Yahweh, their ancestors’ god.
\c 35
\s1 Celebrating the messenger’s ‘passing-over’
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 23:21-23)
\p
\v 1 Then \add King\add* Josiah prepared a ‘pass-over’ celebration in Jerusalem, and they slaughtered the lambs at the end of March.
\v 2 He assigned the priests to the various tasks that needed to be done, and encouraged them to do their work well.
\v 3 He told the Levites (they’d been separated \add from the other tribes\add* in order to serve Yahweh) who were teaching all of Israel, “Place the sacred box in the house that was built by David’s son, Israel’s King Solomon—it won’t be a burden any more for your shoulders. Now serve your god Yahweh and his Israelite people,
\v 4 and prepare yourselves in your clan divisions as per the instructions written by Israel’s King David and his son Solomon.
\v 5 Then stand in the temple area, grouped by your clans and ready to help the other tribes.
\v 6 Consecrate yourselves and slaughter the ‘pass-over’ \add lambs\add* for the people as per the instructions \add given\add* through Moses by Yahweh.
\p
\v 7 \add King\add* Josiah provided thirty thousand lambs and young goats from his own flocks for the ‘pass-over’ \add sacrifices\add*, and three thousand bulls from his own herds.
\v 8 His officials also contributed for \add the benefit of\add* the people, priests, and Levites. The chief temple officials Hilkiyah, Zechariah, and Yehiel donated 2,600 lambs and three hundred cattle to the priests.
\v 9 The Levite leaders Konaniyah and his younger brothers Shemayah and Netanel, along with Hashavyah, Yeiel, and Yozavad, \add together\add* contributed five thousand lambs and three hundred bulls.
\p
\v 10 So everything was prepared as the king had commanded, and the priests stood at their places, and the Levites were there in their \add various\add* divisions,
\v 11 and they slaughtered the ‘pass-over’ \add lambs\add*, then the priests sprinkled the blood while the Levites skinned the animals.
\v 12 They set aside \add the animals\add* to be burnt \add on the altar\add*, in order to give them to the various family groups to offer to Yahweh, following the instructions Moses had written down. They did the same thing with the cattle.
\v 13 They roasted the ‘pass-over’ \add meal\add* over the fire as per the instructions, and they boiled the meat of the sacred offerings in pots and kettles and pans, and served the meat immediately to all the people who were there.
\v 14 Afterwards, the Levites prepared the meal for themselves and for the priests (Aaron’s descendants), because the priests had been busy sacrificing the burnt offerings and the fat portions right through to the evening.
\v 15 The singers (Asaf’s descendants) were at their posts, as they’d been commanded by \add King\add* David, Asaf, Heyman, and the king’s prophet Yedutun. The men guarding the gates didn’t have to leave their posts, because their fellow Levites prepared food for them.
\p
\v 16 So on that day, everything that needed to be done for worshipping Yahweh was done. They celebrated the ‘pass-over’ festival, and they sacrificed burnt offerings as King Josiah had commanded.
\v 17 The Israelites who were there celebrated the ‘pass-over’ that day, then they celebrated the ‘Festival of Flat Bread’ for seven days.
\v 18 There hadn’t been a ‘pass-over’ celebration done like that in Israel since the time of the prophet Samuel, and none of Israel’s kings had ever done it like Josiah did, along with the priests and Levites and the people from Jerusalem and all of Judah and Israel.
\v 19 They celebrated that festival in the eighteenth year of Josiah’s reign.
\s1 The death of Judah’s King Josiah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 23:28-30)
\p
\v 20 After all of Josiah’s work on temple restoration, Egypt’s King Nekoh went to attack Karkemish \add city\add* on the Euphrates \add river\add*, and Josiah went out to confront him.
\v 21 Nekoh sent messengers to tell Josiah, “What’s this got to do with you, King of Judah? We’re not attacking you at present, because we’re in a different fight, and God told me to hurry. Don’t oppose God, who’s with me, and don’t \add make\add* him destroy you.”
\v 22 However, Josiah wouldn’t listen, but disguised himself before going into battle. He didn’t listen to Nekoh even though he was speaking for God, so he went to fight on the Megiddo plain.
\p
\v 23 King Josiah was hit by the archers, and he told his servants, “Get me away from here, because I’m badly wounded.”
\v 24 So his servants lifted him off his chariot and took him on his reserve chariot back to Jerusalem, \add where he died\add*. He was buried in a tomb with his ancestors, and all of Jerusalem and Judah mourned over him.
\p
\v 25 \add The prophet\add* Jeremiah wrote a funeral song for him, which the male and female singers still use at funerals to this day. That became a custom in Israel —the words of that song are written in a scroll of funeral songs.
\p
\v 26 The record of all the other things Josiah did while he was king, and his loyal commitment to following Yahweh’s instructions,
\v 27 including his actions from the beginning to the end \add of his reign\add*, was written on the scroll ‘The kings of Israel and Judah’.
\c 36
\s1 Judah’s King Yehoahaz
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 23:30-35)
\p
\v 1 Then the people of Judah took Josiah’s son Yehoahaz and made him king in Jerusalem to replace his father.
\v 2 Yehoahaz was twenty-three when he became king, and he reigned from Jerusalem for \add only\add* three months.
\v 3 \add Then\add* Egypt’s king deposed him and forced the country to pay an \add annual\add* tribute of thirty kilograms of gold and three tonnes of silver.
\v 4 Egypt’s King Nekoh took Yehoahaz to Egypt, and then the king made his \add younger\add* brother Eliakim king over Judah and Jerusalem, although he changed Eliakim’s name to Yehoyakim.
\s1 Judah’s King Yehoyakim
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 23:36–24:7)
\p
\v 5 Yehoyakim was twenty-five when he became king, and he reigned from Jerusalem for eleven years. He did many things that Yahweh had said were evil.
\v 6 Babylon’s King Nebuchadnezzar attacked \add and captured Yehoyakim\add* and took him to Babylon in bronze chains,
\v 7 along with some items from Yahweh’s temple that were taken to Babylon and placed in the king’s temple there.
\p
\v 8 The record of all the other things Yehoyakim did while he was king, including the disgusting things he did, was written on the scroll ‘The kings of Israel and Judah’, and his son Yehoyakin replaced him as king.
\s1 Judah’s King Yehoyakin
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 24:8-17)
\p
\v 9 Yehoyakin was eighteen\f + \fr 36:9 \ft Using 2 Kings 24:8 to (hopefully) correct the Hebrew here which has ‘eight’.\f* when he became king, and he reigned from Jerusalem for \add just\add* ten days. He did many things that Yahweh had said were evil.
\v 10 At the beginning of the next year, King Nebuchadnezzar sent \add soldiers\add* to bring him to Babylon, bringing valuable items from the temple, and he made his \add younger\add* brother Zedekiah king over Judah and Jerusalem.
\s1 Judah’s king Zedekiah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 24:18-20; Jer. 52:1-3a)
\p
\v 11 Zedekiah was twenty-one when he became king, and he reigned from Jerusalem for eleven years.
\v 12 He did many things that Yahweh had said were evil, \add in fact,\add* he didn’t \add even\add* humble himself when the prophet Jeremiah brought him a message from Yahweh.
\v 13 He’d become very stubborn and wouldn’t return to Israel’s god Yahweh, and he \add also\add* rebelled against King Nebuchadnezzar, who’d made him promise in front of God \add to be loyal\add*.
\v 14 In addition, all the leaders of the priests and the people \add of Judah\add* became more and more unfaithful, doing disgusting things often done in other nations, and then they defiled Yahweh’s residence, which he’d consecrated in Jerusalem.
\p
\v 15 So Yahweh, their ancestors’ god, sent many messages via his prophets, because he felt compassion for his people and his residence,
\v 16 but they mocked God’s messengers and rejected the messages—ridiculing his prophets until Yahweh became so angry that he couldn’t be stopped.
\v 17 So Yahweh sent the Chaldean king against them, and the Chaldeans ran Judah’s young men through with the sword, \add even\add* in the temple, and they didn’t spare any of the young men or women, or \add even\add* those who were older or elderly—God allowed Nebuchadnezzar to \add thoroughly\add* defeat them.
\v 18 Then he took everything valuable, big or small, from God’s temple, as well as from the king and his officials.
\v 19 They set God’s temple on fire, and tore down \add some of\add* the wall \add surrounding\add* Jerusalem, burnt all its palaces, and destroyed everything valuable.
\v 20 Anyone else who was still alive was taken to Babylon, where they became servants to the king and his children until \add the time when\add* the Persians came to power.
\v 21 \add That\add* fulfilled God’s message that Jeremiah had \add previously declared\add*, that the land would become desolate \add to make up for\add* the Rest Days \add that had been ignored\add*—that took seventy years.
\s1 Cyrus sends the exiles back to Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (Ezra 1:1-4)
\p
\v 22 During the first year that King Cyrus reigned over Persia, in order to \add further\add* fulfil what Yahweh had said via \add the prophet\add* Jeremiah, Yahweh worked on the spirit of King Cyrus who then wrote this ruling \add and sent messengers\add* to announce it throughout his kingdom:
\v 23 “I, Persia’s King Cyrus, declare that Yahweh, the god of the heavens, has given me all these earthly kingdoms, and he’s given me the job of building a house for him in Jerusalem, which is in Judah. \add To help with that,\add* anyone who’s from there is allowed to return, and may their god Yahweh be with them and help them.
